WEBVTT


00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.500
Downloaded @ subs4free.club


00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:07.371
My name is Damon.


00:02:09.085 --> 00:02:11.622
And this is my daughter, velvet.


00:02:14.627 --> 00:02:16.617
Are you trying to plant dad?


00:02:16.626 --> 00:02:18.116
She's just turned four.


00:02:21.334 --> 00:02:23.746
Her days are spent
in a happy bubble,


00:02:23.751 --> 00:02:26.208
created by my excellent wife,
Zoe.


00:02:26.209 --> 00:02:27.870
Yeah?


00:02:31.751 --> 00:02:34.913
She's lucky enough that
her major concerns right now


00:02:34.917 --> 00:02:39.739
<i>are numbers... Daddy.
Mummy. Velly.</i>


00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:41.661
So how many pieces is that?


00:02:44.375 --> 00:02:46.411
How to tell
a knock knock joke...


00:02:46.417 --> 00:02:48.032
- Knock, knock.
- Who's there?


00:02:48.042 --> 00:02:49.906
- Unicorn.
- Unicorn who?


00:02:49.916 --> 00:02:52.249
Unicorn jump over the rainbow.


00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:56.162
Oh, that's who it is!


00:02:56.166 --> 00:02:58.908
And the elusive art of
sleep direction.


00:03:03.166 --> 00:03:05.907
But soon she'll have to
leave that happy bubble


00:03:05.915 --> 00:03:08.908
and face a rapidly
deteriorating environment.


00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:12.746
faster than
the scientists predicted.


00:03:12.748 --> 00:03:14.238
We're seeing large waterfalls


00:03:14.248 --> 00:03:15.784
pouring off the side of the ice.


00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:17.872
It's an alarming acceleration.


00:03:17.873 --> 00:03:20.034
The need to address this
is so urgent...


00:03:21.665 --> 00:03:23.279
That it often overwhelms me.


00:03:29.164 --> 00:03:32.861
The simplest way I can explain
our current predicament


00:03:32.872 --> 00:03:36.035
is to briefly channel
my year nine science teacher


00:03:36.039 --> 00:03:38.906
and pretend our house
is the planet.


00:03:42.914 --> 00:03:45.985
Carbon is a miraculous
building block


00:03:45.996 --> 00:03:47.532
and for millions of years,


00:03:47.538 --> 00:03:50.530
our planet has been part of
a natural carbon cycle.


00:03:50.538 --> 00:03:53.530
Some gets released,
some gets stored,


00:03:53.538 --> 00:03:55.153
and if these things
are balanced,


00:03:55.163 --> 00:03:57.027
then equilibrium is maintained.


00:03:58.538 --> 00:04:00.402
But since the
industrial revolution,


00:04:00.412 --> 00:04:02.779
we've broken the balance


00:04:02.788 --> 00:04:04.527
and have been
dramatically increasing


00:04:04.537 --> 00:04:06.903
the release of
the earth's stored carbon


00:04:06.912 --> 00:04:08.527
into our atmosphere.


00:04:16.245 --> 00:04:18.109
For hundreds of thousands of
years,


00:04:18.119 --> 00:04:22.283
we've hovered between about
180 and 280 parts per million


00:04:22.286 --> 00:04:25.028
of carbon dioxide
in the atmosphere.


00:04:25.036 --> 00:04:26.901
But since the
industrial revolution,


00:04:26.911 --> 00:04:29.903
that number has shot up over 40%


00:04:29.911 --> 00:04:32.402
to more than
400 parts per million,


00:04:32.411 --> 00:04:34.867
trapping heat in our atmosphere.


00:04:36.869 --> 00:04:40.360
More than 90% of
the excess heat being trapped


00:04:40.368 --> 00:04:42.359
is absorbed by our oceans.


00:04:43.618 --> 00:04:47.110
The oceans also absorb much
of the extra carbon dioxide,


00:04:47.118 --> 00:04:53.318
and so are now 30% more acidic
than they were 150 years ago.


00:04:53.326 --> 00:04:55.941
And this is impacting
on biodiversity,


00:04:55.950 --> 00:05:00.614
as many sea creatures,
such as oysters or clams,


00:05:00.617 --> 00:05:03.484
are struggling to make
their shells.


00:05:03.492 --> 00:05:06.858
But the increased heat and
overall energy in the system


00:05:06.866 --> 00:05:09.859
also leads to more intense
weather events.


00:05:12.324 --> 00:05:14.486
Hotter air can hold
more moisture,


00:05:14.492 --> 00:05:17.608
so rainfalls, storms,
hurricanes, floods,


00:05:17.616 --> 00:05:20.323
even snowfalls
can become heavier.


00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:28.062
It is true that the climate
has always been changing.


00:05:28.073 --> 00:05:30.110
It just hasn't changed
this rapidly


00:05:30.116 --> 00:05:32.071
in at least 50 million years.


00:05:36.698 --> 00:05:39.360
Unfortunately,
the increase in heat


00:05:39.365 --> 00:05:42.107
also means that our polar caps
and glaciers


00:05:42.115 --> 00:05:43.729
are rapidly melting,


00:05:43.739 --> 00:05:45.570
which causes sea level rise


00:05:45.572 --> 00:05:47.359
that threatens
hundreds of millions of people


00:05:56.863 --> 00:05:59.355
The earth
is our collective home,


00:05:59.364 --> 00:06:02.856
but we're actually renting it
from future generations.


00:06:02.864 --> 00:06:05.696
So we need to not only rapidly
reduce our emissions...


00:06:07.196 --> 00:06:08.857
Oi! Righto!


00:06:11.071 --> 00:06:13.061
But also find ways to sequester


00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:16.359
or draw down
the excess carbon dioxide


00:06:16.363 --> 00:06:18.353
that has already been
put into the system.


00:06:19.738 --> 00:06:23.559
Many scientists believe that
getting down to 350 ppm


00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:25.481
would be a terrific goal.


00:06:36.487 --> 00:06:38.977
So I think we're all
pretty aware


00:06:38.986 --> 00:06:41.194
that when it comes to
predictions of the future,


00:06:41.195 --> 00:06:44.231
they're almost entirely
negative at the moment.


00:06:44.236 --> 00:06:47.068
Any time you open your newsfeed
or social media,


00:06:47.069 --> 00:06:48.855
there's some kind of
doom and gloom story


00:06:48.861 --> 00:06:51.476
about the future of
our environment.


00:06:52.861 --> 00:06:55.476
And as a father, I...


00:06:55.485 --> 00:06:58.477
I think there's room
for a different story.


00:06:58.485 --> 00:07:00.817
A story that focuses
on the solutions


00:07:00.818 --> 00:07:02.934
to some of these problems.


00:07:02.943 --> 00:07:07.811
So my plan is to go out and
find some of these solutions


00:07:07.817 --> 00:07:10.810
and then create a vision
of a different future


00:07:10.818 --> 00:07:12.682
for our daughter.


00:07:12.692 --> 00:07:13.933
What about...


00:07:13.943 --> 00:07:15.978
I want to show her
what the world would look like


00:07:15.984 --> 00:07:19.806
if the solutions I find
were implemented today.


00:07:19.817 --> 00:07:22.980
So, what would the world
look like in 2040


00:07:22.984 --> 00:07:26.351
if we just embraced
the best that already exists?


00:07:27.733 --> 00:07:29.975
And that's my only rule.


00:07:29.984 --> 00:07:32.725
Everything I show her in
this 2040,


00:07:32.733 --> 00:07:36.055
has to exist today,
in some form.


00:07:36.066 --> 00:07:37.556
I can't make it up.


00:07:38.983 --> 00:07:41.349
- See you, dadda.
- See you, darling.


00:07:45.482 --> 00:07:46.689
Have fun.


00:07:47.733 --> 00:07:50.064
I'm calling it
an exercise in


00:07:50.065 --> 00:07:52.432
fact-based dreaming.


00:08:17.813 --> 00:08:22.933
The first step in this exercise
was to consult the generation


00:08:22.938 --> 00:08:26.601
who'll be sharing the future
with our daughter.


00:08:28.105 --> 00:08:29.596
- Hi, everybody.
- Morning.


00:08:29.606 --> 00:08:31.470
Morning. My name is Damon.


00:08:31.480 --> 00:08:33.721
Now, do you guys know where
we've come from today?


00:08:33.730 --> 00:08:35.686
Do you know what country
that I'm from?


00:08:35.688 --> 00:08:36.598
No.


00:08:36.605 --> 00:08:38.845
We have animals called platypus.


00:08:40.730 --> 00:08:43.686
- Kangaroo.
- Australia!


00:08:43.687 --> 00:08:45.177
Australia? Yeah, yeah, yeah.


00:08:45.187 --> 00:08:47.098
We've been going
all around the world


00:08:47.104 --> 00:08:48.844
and talking to lots of
different children your age,


00:08:48.854 --> 00:08:50.560
and so we're going to
ask you what kind of things


00:08:50.562 --> 00:08:52.052
you want to see in the future.


00:08:55.270 --> 00:08:59.263
Well, what would make me
happy in the world is...


00:08:59.270 --> 00:09:00.760
Hmm.


00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:03.635
I think we should get
this invention


00:09:03.645 --> 00:09:09.015
which sucks up
all of the rubbish in the world


00:09:09.020 --> 00:09:12.591
and puts it in a intergalactic
dimension,


00:09:12.602 --> 00:09:15.140
which is a rubbish dimension.


00:09:15.145 --> 00:09:18.341
I'd like to see deforestation
being stopped


00:09:18.352 --> 00:09:21.219
because it's ruining the planet.


00:09:21.227 --> 00:09:23.968
Animals are losing their homes.


00:09:33.643 --> 00:09:37.135
<i>Cleaner water. That would
really make me happy.</i>


00:09:45.225 --> 00:09:47.387
Well, I would like for
the government


00:09:47.393 --> 00:09:51.466
to have done something on
global warming and pollution,


00:09:51.476 --> 00:09:55.889
as now, I think they're not
really doing anything about it.


00:09:55.892 --> 00:09:58.008
You know,
just be respectful to earth.


00:10:03.517 --> 00:10:07.213
It was sobering to learn
how preoccupied the kids are


00:10:07.224 --> 00:10:09.511
with the state of the planet,


00:10:09.517 --> 00:10:11.507
and what a big job
I had ahead of me.


00:10:12.641 --> 00:10:16.463
So I began my search
for solutions with energy.


00:10:43.222 --> 00:10:47.636
I found myself in Bangladesh,
where the roads are chaotic...


00:10:49.514 --> 00:10:52.256
And people are openly
peddling ice on the streets.


00:10:54.764 --> 00:10:57.346
Currently, Bangladesh
has one of the largest


00:10:57.347 --> 00:10:59.258
solar home system
markets in the world.


00:10:59.264 --> 00:11:01.003
Over 5 million
solar home systems.


00:11:01.013 --> 00:11:03.845
So most households in every
rural area in Bangladesh


00:11:03.846 --> 00:11:05.336
actually has
a solar home system,


00:11:05.346 --> 00:11:07.132
and that's their primary
source of energy.


00:11:07.138 --> 00:11:08.469
Wow.


00:11:09.971 --> 00:11:14.385
This charming 23-year-old
is neel tamhane,


00:11:14.388 --> 00:11:17.094
who has studied alternative
energy technologies


00:11:17.095 --> 00:11:19.883
and come home to help
power his country.


00:11:21.637 --> 00:11:25.505
Turns out I wasn't the only one
who wanted to hear him talk.


00:11:27.845 --> 00:11:31.008
So what we do is we
interconnect solar home systems


00:11:31.012 --> 00:11:33.627
and we enable energy
sharing between them,


00:11:33.636 --> 00:11:35.127
which is basically trading,


00:11:35.137 --> 00:11:37.468
and the customer chooses
when they want to trade


00:11:37.469 --> 00:11:38.959
and when they don't.


00:11:40.512 --> 00:11:42.877
Instead of building large grids,


00:11:42.886 --> 00:11:45.127
we're proposing
a decentralised structure


00:11:45.136 --> 00:11:47.969
where we start building grids
bottom up.


00:11:50.135 --> 00:11:53.253
The setup
is quite simple.


00:11:53.261 --> 00:11:56.002
Any home that has solar panels
and a battery...


00:11:57.885 --> 00:11:59.750
Can buy this special box...


00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:03.000
Which connects them
to another house


00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:04.500
with the same setup.


00:12:06.467 --> 00:12:10.006
The box allows the buying
or selling of energy


00:12:10.010 --> 00:12:11.499
between the homes.


00:12:12.759 --> 00:12:14.216
But it gets better.


00:12:15.467 --> 00:12:17.958
There is still a segment
of the population


00:12:17.967 --> 00:12:19.958
that still can't afford to
buy a solar home system.


00:12:19.967 --> 00:12:21.503
So, instead of doing that,


00:12:21.509 --> 00:12:24.091
if they can just buy
a small solbox,


00:12:24.092 --> 00:12:26.003
they can just buy energy
when they need it.


00:12:26.009 --> 00:12:28.590
You fill up your solbox
with money.


00:12:28.591 --> 00:12:31.503
As you keep using energy,
it deducts money.


00:12:36.466 --> 00:12:38.252
What this means


00:12:38.258 --> 00:12:40.624
is that all the boxes
can connect to each other


00:12:40.633 --> 00:12:42.123
to form a microgrid.


00:12:43.507 --> 00:12:45.874
It's like a water tank
of community energy


00:12:45.883 --> 00:12:48.840
that people can give to
or take from.


00:12:50.465 --> 00:12:52.581
The beauty is that
this microgrid


00:12:52.590 --> 00:12:55.206
can then connect to the
adjacent village's microgrid


00:12:55.215 --> 00:12:58.207
and the network becomes
stronger and stronger.


00:12:59.757 --> 00:13:01.998
There's a mimicking of nature
here, isn't there?


00:13:02.007 --> 00:13:05.123
Like, in the way that
cells multiply


00:13:05.131 --> 00:13:07.589
and form something
and strengthen it.


00:13:07.590 --> 00:13:09.580
Yeah, that's a great analogy.


00:13:09.589 --> 00:13:11.875
You have one solar home system,


00:13:11.881 --> 00:13:13.963
you interconnect with your
neighbours, you make it 50.


00:13:13.964 --> 00:13:15.750
Slowly,
you interconnect villages.


00:13:15.756 --> 00:13:17.336
Once you have collected
hundreds,


00:13:17.338 --> 00:13:19.330
you can hook it up to the grid,
you can sell to the grid.


00:13:19.339 --> 00:13:20.828
Forget buying from it -


00:13:20.838 --> 00:13:22.624
you become the primary
energy generation source


00:13:22.630 --> 00:13:24.121
for the country.


00:13:25.130 --> 00:13:27.371
So the idea is
we are like a swarm of bees


00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:30.497
or a swarm of fish
that move together,


00:13:30.505 --> 00:13:33.462
pool in all our energies
together to run bigger loads.


00:13:35.838 --> 00:13:38.624
This technology
could revolutionise


00:13:38.629 --> 00:13:41.746
the way we distribute energy
worldwide.


00:13:44.087 --> 00:13:46.954
From little things,
big things grow.


00:13:48.629 --> 00:13:52.827
When we have a
decentralised mode of energy,


00:13:52.837 --> 00:13:54.622
the same thing that happened
with the web,


00:13:54.628 --> 00:13:56.118
happens with information


00:13:56.128 --> 00:13:58.120
where basically,
it's Democratic.


00:13:58.129 --> 00:13:59.868
Energy becomes Democratic.


00:13:59.878 --> 00:14:02.245
But also it becomes
very efficient.


00:14:02.254 --> 00:14:03.709
It's far more efficient


00:14:03.711 --> 00:14:07.123
because you produce it at
the point where you consume it.


00:14:11.335 --> 00:14:14.499
Having efficient
local energy


00:14:14.503 --> 00:14:17.118
also provides greater resilience


00:14:17.127 --> 00:14:19.584
in an increasingly
hostile climate.


00:14:21.710 --> 00:14:24.122
And when we talk about
climate change, it's floods,


00:14:24.127 --> 00:14:27.368
it's natural disasters
that happen very often here.


00:14:27.376 --> 00:14:31.120
<i>In the last 1.5 years, we have
seen about 5, 6 disasters.</i>


00:14:34.334 --> 00:14:37.576
When you have a centralised
grid, everything breaks down


00:14:37.584 --> 00:14:40.451
and then it takes a long time
to build it back up.


00:14:43.584 --> 00:14:46.075
If we have decentralised
generation sources,


00:14:46.084 --> 00:14:48.620
every household has their own
generation source.


00:14:48.625 --> 00:14:49.740
They're independent


00:14:49.750 --> 00:14:51.241
and they have their power
in their own hands,


00:14:51.251 --> 00:14:52.740
instead of depending
on the government


00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:54.365
to provide it to them.


00:15:13.999 --> 00:15:17.320
The impact of the
locally-generated electricity


00:15:17.331 --> 00:15:19.118
is best seen at night.


00:15:21.748 --> 00:15:26.572
The influx of cash has made the
village bazaar the place to be.


00:16:03.704 --> 00:16:05.990
You guys have just skipped
the landline telephone.


00:16:05.996 --> 00:16:07.486
You've gone straight to
the mobile.


00:16:07.496 --> 00:16:09.987
<i>Exactly. And it's throughout.
It's not even about energy.</i>


00:16:09.996 --> 00:16:12.577
We skipped the grid
and we build our own.


00:16:12.578 --> 00:16:15.116
There are developing
countries like India,


00:16:15.121 --> 00:16:17.327
Bangladesh, a lot
of countries in east Africa


00:16:17.328 --> 00:16:19.694
that are trying to do
a top-down electrification.


00:16:19.703 --> 00:16:21.819
They are spending
billions of dollars here.


00:16:21.828 --> 00:16:23.318
But instead of doing that,


00:16:23.328 --> 00:16:25.740
if they can actually help
subsidise these systems


00:16:25.745 --> 00:16:27.951
and let people
own their own system,


00:16:27.952 --> 00:16:30.990
they would be so proud
by owning these.


00:16:30.995 --> 00:16:33.610
So when I'm trading energy
with you, suppose,


00:16:33.619 --> 00:16:36.941
you're actually paying me for
it, and I'm getting the money,


00:16:36.952 --> 00:16:39.068
so the money also stays
within the economy.


00:16:41.244 --> 00:16:42.950
This is bringing back people
together.


00:16:42.952 --> 00:16:45.237
Yes!


00:16:47.994 --> 00:16:52.567
As I left the village,
I had a renewed sense of hope.


00:16:52.577 --> 00:16:55.113
Particularly when neel told me


00:16:55.118 --> 00:16:57.450
this technology is springing up
in other countries.


00:17:01.367 --> 00:17:04.325
This solution is
great for our environment,


00:17:04.326 --> 00:17:07.739
with so many cascading benefits


00:17:07.743 --> 00:17:10.609
for any community
that chooses to adopt it.


00:17:16.992 --> 00:17:21.486
So, sticking to my rule of only
showing what exists today,


00:17:21.492 --> 00:17:24.609
here's what the future
of energy could look like


00:17:24.617 --> 00:17:26.106
for my daughter.


00:17:34.824 --> 00:17:37.031
This, velvet, could be you.


00:17:38.074 --> 00:17:39.438
Or this.


00:17:40.532 --> 00:17:42.193
Or even this.


00:17:42.199 --> 00:17:44.154
We love you.


00:17:44.156 --> 00:17:46.818
And this could be where
you live.


00:17:48.073 --> 00:17:49.313
Or here.


00:17:51.698 --> 00:17:53.689
Or even here.


00:17:53.698 --> 00:17:55.562
But probably not here.


00:17:55.572 --> 00:18:00.020
<i>Darling! It's seven o'clock!
It's time for family yoga!</i>


00:18:04.947 --> 00:18:06.653
Crystal, bring up energy.


00:18:07.822 --> 00:18:10.062
<i>Certainly.
What would you like me to do?</i>


00:18:11.447 --> 00:18:14.314
Imagine if your house
in 2040


00:18:14.322 --> 00:18:16.061
is part of a microgrid


00:18:16.071 --> 00:18:19.269
that helps power the economy
throughout your city.


00:18:25.196 --> 00:18:27.687
Imagine if your windows
are solar glass


00:18:27.696 --> 00:18:29.812
and come standard in new homes.


00:18:31.446 --> 00:18:35.644
And your solar battery
is cheap and recyclable.


00:18:37.653 --> 00:18:41.191
This could allow you to
donate your excess energy.


00:18:41.195 --> 00:18:43.151
I'm going away for four days.


00:18:43.153 --> 00:18:45.269
Would you like to
share or sell?


00:18:45.278 --> 00:18:48.520
Share. Hurricane relief.


00:18:48.528 --> 00:18:50.563
Done. You're a legend.


00:18:50.569 --> 00:18:52.310
Thanks.


00:18:52.320 --> 00:18:53.934
- Humans are awesome.
- Ok.


00:18:53.944 --> 00:18:56.687
That choice
was good for your soul.


00:18:56.695 --> 00:18:58.025
Alright.


00:18:59.069 --> 00:19:00.809
Every home
is going to have a battery


00:19:00.819 --> 00:19:03.401
that's going to store
as much electricity


00:19:03.402 --> 00:19:04.892
as it possibly can store.


00:19:04.902 --> 00:19:07.063
And all the governments
in the world


00:19:07.069 --> 00:19:08.934
cannot push back against that


00:19:08.944 --> 00:19:11.434
because folks are going to go
buy it at the store,


00:19:11.443 --> 00:19:14.560
at Ikea, at Walmart,
at the supermarket.


00:19:14.568 --> 00:19:16.058
And it's going to be very cheap.


00:19:16.068 --> 00:19:18.060
So cheap that you're not even
going to notice.


00:19:22.692 --> 00:19:25.025
If cheaper
home energy systems


00:19:25.026 --> 00:19:28.017
are combined with more people
demanding clean energy


00:19:28.025 --> 00:19:29.516
from their politicians,
darling...


00:19:30.942 --> 00:19:32.557
Then there's a chance
many countries


00:19:32.567 --> 00:19:36.560
could be close to
100% renewable by 2040.


00:19:40.942 --> 00:19:42.432
What I can guarantee


00:19:42.442 --> 00:19:44.932
is that not only will I be
an embarrassing dad...


00:19:44.941 --> 00:19:46.432
Find news.


00:19:47.524 --> 00:19:50.311
But that natural disasters
will increase their intensity


00:19:50.316 --> 00:19:52.022
over the next two decades.


00:19:53.649 --> 00:19:56.061
Absolute chaos.


00:19:56.066 --> 00:19:58.773
<i>Run, run! Get out!
Run, run!</i>


00:20:01.190 --> 00:20:03.432
God. Oh, my god!


00:20:04.898 --> 00:20:06.434
Whoa!


00:20:13.065 --> 00:20:14.646
But what I now know


00:20:14.648 --> 00:20:17.559
is that we actually have
everything we need


00:20:17.564 --> 00:20:20.307
on both a large and small-scale


00:20:20.315 --> 00:20:23.307
to power us through
these disasters.


00:20:24.565 --> 00:20:26.304
And by building new grids,


00:20:26.314 --> 00:20:29.636
loads of people
will get loads of jobs.


00:20:30.814 --> 00:20:32.554
- Oh, that's good.
- Mmm.


00:20:34.272 --> 00:20:38.059
But what would be really great
during this transition, velvet,


00:20:38.063 --> 00:20:41.260
is if the people who work
in the fossil fuel industry


00:20:41.271 --> 00:20:45.640
are given support and funding
for retraining in new careers.


00:20:47.271 --> 00:20:50.763
Paid for by redirecting some of
the $10 million a minute


00:20:50.771 --> 00:20:54.934
governments currently spend
subsidising fossil fuels.


00:21:20.019 --> 00:21:22.806
What struck me
about the microgrids


00:21:22.810 --> 00:21:25.643
is that the profits from
the shared energy


00:21:25.644 --> 00:21:29.011
stay within the community
and empower individuals,


00:21:29.019 --> 00:21:32.181
rather than going to a big
energy company elsewhere.


00:21:34.018 --> 00:21:37.260
But it turns out microgrids
are currently illegal


00:21:37.268 --> 00:21:38.758
in some countries.


00:21:41.268 --> 00:21:43.929
If we recognised
that our well-being


00:21:43.934 --> 00:21:46.517
fundamentally depends
upon the stability


00:21:46.518 --> 00:21:48.428
and the thriving of this planet,


00:21:48.434 --> 00:21:49.924
we would put that at the heart


00:21:49.934 --> 00:21:51.425
of the economic system
we create.


00:21:53.309 --> 00:21:55.800
I met with
the economist Kate raworth


00:21:55.809 --> 00:21:57.515
in an appropriate location.


00:21:59.434 --> 00:22:01.925
Kate is proposing
a new economic framework


00:22:01.934 --> 00:22:04.266
designed for our current
predicament.


00:22:05.683 --> 00:22:08.800
Today's economy,
the returns of production


00:22:08.808 --> 00:22:12.390
are accruing to a 1%,
which leaves us with


00:22:12.392 --> 00:22:14.131
these extraordinary levels
of inequality.


00:22:14.141 --> 00:22:15.802
So we need to create an economy


00:22:15.808 --> 00:22:18.640
in which value created
is shared far more equitably


00:22:18.641 --> 00:22:20.427
with all those
who help create it.


00:22:20.433 --> 00:22:23.550
And daft though it sounds, I
think it looks like a doughnut.


00:22:23.558 --> 00:22:25.422
The kind with the hole
in the middle.


00:22:25.432 --> 00:22:28.265
So, in the hole in the middle
of that doughnut


00:22:29.807 --> 00:22:33.423
on life's essentials
- be it food,


00:22:33.432 --> 00:22:40.423
housing, education,
water, energy,


00:22:40.432 --> 00:22:43.264
and so we want to get everybody
in the world out of that hole.


00:22:45.432 --> 00:22:49.504
But we also can't go beyond
the doughnut's outer crust,


00:22:49.514 --> 00:22:51.505
because there we start to put
so much pressure


00:22:51.514 --> 00:22:53.004
on this extraordinary planet,


00:22:53.014 --> 00:22:54.753
causing climate breakdown...


00:22:56.431 --> 00:22:57.762
Biodiversity loss...


00:22:59.555 --> 00:23:00.795
Air-pollution...


00:23:02.264 --> 00:23:03.878
Too much land conversion.


00:23:04.805 --> 00:23:07.762
We kick out of kilter this
extraordinary living planet


00:23:07.763 --> 00:23:09.628
on which all of our well-being
depends.


00:23:12.930 --> 00:23:15.295
Last century's economists
didn't see this.


00:23:15.304 --> 00:23:18.047
No phase of humanity
has encountered this before.


00:23:18.055 --> 00:23:20.420
It's our generational challenge.


00:23:20.429 --> 00:23:22.044
We need new ideas to do this.


00:23:25.054 --> 00:23:28.751
The wonderful thing about
the microgrids


00:23:28.762 --> 00:23:32.049
is that they fit beautifully
within the doughnut framework.


00:23:33.178 --> 00:23:36.420
The solar energy helps restore
the outer boundaries


00:23:36.428 --> 00:23:37.918
like climate change...


00:23:39.011 --> 00:23:40.421
And air-pollution...


00:23:41.928 --> 00:23:43.634
But on the inner boundaries,


00:23:43.636 --> 00:23:46.548
more people are pulled
into the doughnut


00:23:46.553 --> 00:23:49.419
by improving health,
with less kerosene use...


00:23:50.761 --> 00:23:53.627
Education, by providing light
to study...


00:23:55.135 --> 00:23:56.421
Income equality,


00:23:56.428 --> 00:23:58.634
by keeping profits within
the local economy...


00:23:59.927 --> 00:24:01.292
And networks,


00:24:01.302 --> 00:24:04.374
because the microgrid now
interconnects households.


00:24:20.176 --> 00:24:23.794
I am going to invent a plane
and...


00:24:23.801 --> 00:24:25.791
You type in the coordinates
where...


00:24:25.800 --> 00:24:29.918
<i>What you want it... where you
want it to go and...</i>


00:24:29.925 --> 00:24:32.792
In a split second, you're there.


00:24:32.800 --> 00:24:35.507
I think electric cars
would help the environment


00:24:35.508 --> 00:24:37.794
because that causes
less pollution.


00:24:37.800 --> 00:24:42.543
And maybe, um, get electric
cars if they can, um,


00:24:42.549 --> 00:24:45.041
'cause that reduces,
like, fossil fuels


00:24:45.050 --> 00:24:47.040
and then that also stops
global warming.


00:24:47.049 --> 00:24:48.789
And instead of using cars,


00:24:48.799 --> 00:24:51.040
I would like to use
rocket boots to go around


00:24:51.049 --> 00:24:53.256
and they would be
powered by plants.


00:24:53.257 --> 00:24:56.044
<i>Oh, that would be so cool.
That would be awesome.</i>


00:24:56.049 --> 00:24:57.505
And rocket boots, I think,


00:24:57.507 --> 00:25:00.919
would really cool to see people
flying around the city in.


00:25:00.923 --> 00:25:03.006
And also I want it to go
worldwide


00:25:03.007 --> 00:25:05.042
and I want to try
and invent them.


00:25:12.673 --> 00:25:14.629
So, it's interesting


00:25:14.631 --> 00:25:16.666
being in some of these
big American cities,


00:25:16.672 --> 00:25:18.288
the traffic is just diabolical.


00:25:18.298 --> 00:25:20.128
And I can see that it...


00:25:20.130 --> 00:25:22.998
You know, I consider myself
quite a happy person, you know.


00:25:23.006 --> 00:25:24.916
I've got not a lot to
complain about.


00:25:26.047 --> 00:25:27.537
But I'm really...


00:25:29.005 --> 00:25:32.622
Feeling a bit antsy
in this traffic and, um...


00:25:32.629 --> 00:25:34.040
It kind of does that to you.


00:25:34.047 --> 00:25:37.038
And I think probably
a lot of people


00:25:37.046 --> 00:25:39.162
might not put that as a factor


00:25:39.171 --> 00:25:41.036
because they've got other
things going on in their life,


00:25:41.046 --> 00:25:42.536
but if you do have things
in your life


00:25:42.546 --> 00:25:44.036
that are bothering you,


00:25:44.046 --> 00:25:46.128
then you throw yourself
into this environment...


00:25:46.129 --> 00:25:47.790
Things can get a little tense.


00:25:47.796 --> 00:25:49.786
And I've been tempted to
jump on the horn


00:25:49.795 --> 00:25:51.536
and abuse couple of people.


00:25:59.545 --> 00:26:01.251
In the us right now,


00:26:01.253 --> 00:26:05.041
road vehicles contribute
to 20% of emissions.


00:26:07.920 --> 00:26:12.163
But our environment and sanity
will be severely tested


00:26:12.170 --> 00:26:15.286
with predictions of an extra
one billion cars worldwide


00:26:15.294 --> 00:26:16.784
by 2040.


00:26:19.919 --> 00:26:21.875
# I can lock all my doors... #


00:26:21.877 --> 00:26:25.916
a possible solution could be
replacing car ownership


00:26:25.919 --> 00:26:28.501
with on-demand
driverless vehicles.


00:26:32.043 --> 00:26:35.535
The same way that we
went from owning DVDs or cds


00:26:35.543 --> 00:26:37.124
or records or whatever,


00:26:37.126 --> 00:26:39.287
to having all of those
on demand 


00:26:39.293 --> 00:26:41.500
essentially Netflix and so on 


00:26:41.501 --> 00:26:45.164
the same thing is going to
happen with transportation.


00:26:47.417 --> 00:26:49.533
I wasn't sure I'd trust
a driverless car


00:26:49.542 --> 00:26:51.373
with my daughter,


00:26:51.375 --> 00:26:53.036
so I subjected this prototype


00:26:53.042 --> 00:26:55.533
to the surprise pedestrian test.


00:26:58.250 --> 00:27:00.114
Ah! Yes!


00:27:01.917 --> 00:27:04.123
Robots - 1, human - 0.


00:27:06.667 --> 00:27:09.282
The next test was
a little more challenging.


00:27:10.416 --> 00:27:11.781
Traffic.


00:27:13.041 --> 00:27:14.531
When I push this button,


00:27:14.541 --> 00:27:16.656
this car will be driving itself.


00:27:16.665 --> 00:27:18.531
And, um, wish me luck.


00:27:26.998 --> 00:27:28.284
Right.


00:27:31.040 --> 00:27:34.612
So, this is my first experience
in a driverless vehicle,


00:27:34.623 --> 00:27:35.988
and, uh...


00:27:37.622 --> 00:27:39.159
I'm a little bit nervous


00:27:39.165 --> 00:27:41.155
because that wheel
is turning by itself.


00:27:42.664 --> 00:27:44.621
Siri, please look after me.


00:27:45.997 --> 00:27:48.784
So, yeah, you can see
that's our car tracking there,


00:27:48.789 --> 00:27:51.656
and the little lidar,
the radar on top


00:27:51.664 --> 00:27:53.619
is reading out
all the information


00:27:53.621 --> 00:27:56.034
I think, about 50m ahead of us


00:27:56.039 --> 00:27:59.906
and scanning the area and
determining what's around us,


00:27:59.913 --> 00:28:02.404
what other obstacles,
what it should look out for -


00:28:02.413 --> 00:28:05.780
pedestrians, bike riders,
other cars.


00:28:05.788 --> 00:28:08.154
So it's constantly
ahead of us, predicting.


00:28:09.538 --> 00:28:11.653
Two things
are going to happen.


00:28:11.662 --> 00:28:14.529
We're all going to have
a massive fleet of cars


00:28:14.537 --> 00:28:17.530
that are going to pick you
up at home, take you to work,


00:28:17.538 --> 00:28:19.493
take you to the supermarket
and so on,


00:28:19.495 --> 00:28:21.031
and the other one -


00:28:21.037 --> 00:28:25.110
even if you already own a car
that you already paid off,


00:28:25.120 --> 00:28:28.783
it's going to be four times
more expensive


00:28:28.787 --> 00:28:31.027
just to own that car


00:28:31.036 --> 00:28:33.779
than it is to access
transportation as a service,


00:28:33.787 --> 00:28:35.651
so, autonomous
electric vehicles.


00:28:39.744 --> 00:28:42.907
I do like the freedom
of my own car,


00:28:42.911 --> 00:28:44.776
so the big question is


00:28:44.786 --> 00:28:47.027
are enough of us
willing to give that up


00:28:47.036 --> 00:28:49.492
and embrace shared
transportation?


00:28:51.161 --> 00:28:52.650
One of the interesting
challenges


00:28:52.660 --> 00:28:55.528
about a future of vehicles
that involves ridesharing,


00:28:55.536 --> 00:28:57.742
is that we have all these ideas
tied up with owning vehicles


00:28:57.743 --> 00:28:59.233
that have very little to do with


00:28:59.243 --> 00:29:00.652
getting us from
point a to point b,


00:29:00.659 --> 00:29:03.367
and all to do with what those
things said about us.


00:29:03.368 --> 00:29:06.154
So, you know, they became
symbols of independence


00:29:06.159 --> 00:29:07.525
and freedom.


00:29:07.535 --> 00:29:10.981
They became symbols of class
and wealth and capital.


00:29:10.992 --> 00:29:14.029
Now, the reality is, of course,
we haven't had cars forever.


00:29:14.034 --> 00:29:16.025
And the notion of cars
as status symbols


00:29:16.034 --> 00:29:17.524
and signals of independence -


00:29:17.534 --> 00:29:19.490
that's 60, 70 years old,
at most,


00:29:19.492 --> 00:29:21.277
and less than that
in most places.


00:29:21.283 --> 00:29:25.276
And so the idea that we
can uncouple those things


00:29:25.283 --> 00:29:26.523
isn't impossible.


00:29:26.533 --> 00:29:28.490
And frankly, they only ever got
coupled in the first place


00:29:28.492 --> 00:29:30.027
because of advertising.


00:29:32.783 --> 00:29:36.526
If we did embrace
on-demand transport,


00:29:36.533 --> 00:29:40.276
much like we now require
fewer cds or DVDs...


00:29:42.282 --> 00:29:44.399
We'd also require
fewer vehicles...


00:29:47.240 --> 00:29:49.526
And almost no parking space.


00:29:53.282 --> 00:29:57.651
Two thirds of la
is parking and roads.


00:29:58.739 --> 00:30:02.778
You could fit three cities
the size of San Francisco


00:30:02.781 --> 00:30:05.523
in the empty parking space


00:30:05.531 --> 00:30:09.399
left by the autonomous vehicle
disruption.


00:30:10.489 --> 00:30:13.606
And so, as a society,
we need to make decisions.


00:30:13.614 --> 00:30:15.899
What do we do
with all that parking space


00:30:15.905 --> 00:30:17.771
that's going to be vacant?


00:30:23.031 --> 00:30:26.397
So, here's what 2040 could
look like for my daughter


00:30:26.405 --> 00:30:29.238
without an extra billion cars.


00:30:37.737 --> 00:30:40.023
As you head off to your first
school dance, velvet...


00:30:40.029 --> 00:30:42.111
Family hug. Family hug.


00:30:42.112 --> 00:30:43.851
It will be nice
not having to worry


00:30:43.861 --> 00:30:45.602
about your date's
driving skills.


00:30:47.279 --> 00:30:52.147
<i>Look, dad! A ghost must be
driving! Spooky!</i>


00:30:56.279 --> 00:30:58.269
I imagine your date
to be very kind.


00:30:59.653 --> 00:31:01.234
And very impotent.


00:31:04.735 --> 00:31:06.727
All the cars that
your mum and I ever owned


00:31:06.736 --> 00:31:08.397
were stranded assets,


00:31:08.403 --> 00:31:12.521
meaning that 96% of the time
they were parked or unused.


00:31:12.528 --> 00:31:14.108
But now, in 2040,


00:31:14.110 --> 00:31:17.398
with driver costs gone
and the rideshare explosion,


00:31:17.402 --> 00:31:20.109
fewer people actually own cars.


00:31:20.110 --> 00:31:23.273
They get around in
luxurious pods like this


00:31:23.277 --> 00:31:25.142
for next to nothing.


00:31:26.609 --> 00:31:29.976
It's just inner cities that are
likely to be driverless only.


00:31:31.026 --> 00:31:33.017
And we could really
fatten the doughnut


00:31:33.026 --> 00:31:35.108
if vehicles were
community-owned.


00:31:40.401 --> 00:31:42.015
But I secretly hope, darling,


00:31:42.025 --> 00:31:44.893
that rocket boots girl
has achieved her ambition


00:31:44.901 --> 00:31:47.607
of worldwide domination.


00:31:47.608 --> 00:31:49.519
So, this is
a huge disruption


00:31:49.525 --> 00:31:52.232
of a massive part of
the labour force.


00:31:52.233 --> 00:31:55.396
Inevitably, there'll be
debates around this technology.


00:31:56.650 --> 00:31:59.481
But if enough of us
embrace ridesharing,


00:31:59.482 --> 00:32:04.021
we could reclaim our cities
for humans instead of vehicles,


00:32:04.024 --> 00:32:06.641
and generate
millions of new jobs.


00:32:24.106 --> 00:32:26.097
This is big vision.


00:32:26.106 --> 00:32:28.518
We just need to have
that vision now.


00:32:28.523 --> 00:32:30.764
A lot of people want to be
working on something


00:32:30.773 --> 00:32:32.763
that they can see is actually
helping to regenerate


00:32:32.772 --> 00:32:35.515
and innovatively
recreate the world.


00:32:39.398 --> 00:32:42.639
The extra space in our
cities could allow the building


00:32:42.647 --> 00:32:45.389
of more low-cost
sustainable homes.


00:32:48.771 --> 00:32:53.595
And we could see the growth
of remanufacturing industries,


00:32:53.604 --> 00:32:56.597
which might convert
existing vehicles to electric.


00:33:01.146 --> 00:33:04.263
But most exciting could be
the urban food farms


00:33:04.271 --> 00:33:06.387
that spring up
in empty parking lots.


00:33:09.270 --> 00:33:11.512
Or inside disused car parks.


00:33:13.853 --> 00:33:17.392
And imagine having fresh local
food growing on our rooftops.


00:33:20.645 --> 00:33:24.638
This would also contribute to
a much healthier environment.


00:33:26.519 --> 00:33:28.886
It's unbeatable to grow
food in your own backyard.


00:33:28.895 --> 00:33:30.384
It's unbeatable to grow food


00:33:30.394 --> 00:33:32.225
all through and around
your city.


00:33:32.227 --> 00:33:35.515
It's unbeatable to grow
food on your roof.


00:33:35.519 --> 00:33:40.593
Those are the absolute
gold standard of emissions.


00:33:44.269 --> 00:33:47.100
One day you will be
amazed by the distances


00:33:47.101 --> 00:33:49.012
some of our food travelled.


00:33:51.476 --> 00:33:53.341
Bring up data for 2016.


00:33:54.768 --> 00:33:59.215
In 2016, the us was importing
the same amount of beef


00:33:59.225 --> 00:34:00.841
as it was exporting.


00:34:06.518 --> 00:34:09.634
Fish caught in Norway was flown
to China for filleting.


00:34:11.725 --> 00:34:13.966
Then flown back to Norway
to be sold.


00:34:16.266 --> 00:34:17.882
What were you guys thinking?


00:34:18.892 --> 00:34:20.381
Well, sometimes we weren't.


00:34:21.474 --> 00:34:23.136
But how's this?


00:34:24.224 --> 00:34:26.761
If we electrify
our transport systems


00:34:26.766 --> 00:34:29.382
and frankly, make them
far more attractive...


00:34:30.641 --> 00:34:34.008
A lot more fossil fuels
will stay in the ground.


00:34:35.266 --> 00:34:36.755
If nobody's going to buy


00:34:36.765 --> 00:34:38.882
internal combustion engine
petrol cars,


00:34:38.891 --> 00:34:41.006
nobody's going to buy oil.


00:34:41.015 --> 00:34:45.884
What we see is that oil demand
is going to peak


00:34:45.890 --> 00:34:49.553
and it's going to
go down dramatically.


00:34:49.557 --> 00:34:51.297
And it's never coming back.


00:34:53.556 --> 00:34:57.004
This means areas like
the tar sands oil fields


00:34:57.015 --> 00:34:59.926
could be magnificently
transformed.


00:35:09.556 --> 00:35:12.798
If you do choose to
live in a city, velvet,


00:35:12.806 --> 00:35:14.420
I hope it's a bit like this.


00:35:18.014 --> 00:35:21.050
A city where
so many of the public parks,


00:35:21.055 --> 00:35:26.254
urban food projects
and cleaner transport networks,


00:35:26.263 --> 00:35:29.130
all contribute to
a healthier environment,


00:35:29.138 --> 00:35:31.549
while fostering a greater sense
of community..


00:35:33.180 --> 00:35:35.545
And a hell of a lot less
road rage.


00:35:44.762 --> 00:35:46.252
But the bonus


00:35:46.262 --> 00:35:49.425
is that you can now actually
hear birds singing


00:35:49.429 --> 00:35:51.135
in the middle of the city.


00:36:03.803 --> 00:36:06.670
As wonderful as
this future could be,


00:36:06.678 --> 00:36:10.044
I was pretty gobsmacked
to learn that vested interests


00:36:10.052 --> 00:36:13.044
now spend almost
$1 billion a year


00:36:13.052 --> 00:36:15.885
preventing us from actually
lowering our emissions.


00:36:19.552 --> 00:36:21.758
So, the fossil fuel industry


00:36:21.759 --> 00:36:23.546
have being doing
a magnificent job


00:36:23.552 --> 00:36:26.008
at copying the tobacco playbook


00:36:26.009 --> 00:36:29.422
in creating doubt and confusion
amongst the public.


00:36:29.426 --> 00:36:31.792
And I've been following
some of their key players,


00:36:31.801 --> 00:36:33.883
their sneaky players, online,


00:36:33.884 --> 00:36:35.420
just to try and learn
and understand


00:36:35.426 --> 00:36:36.916
some of the tactics.


00:36:36.926 --> 00:36:38.415
So, one of the things they do


00:36:38.425 --> 00:36:39.916
is that they create websites


00:36:39.926 --> 00:36:42.132
that sound and look
very Sincere,


00:36:42.133 --> 00:36:44.045
but are full of misinformation.


00:36:44.051 --> 00:36:47.622
You see here the innocent
hummingbird, the leaf.


00:36:47.633 --> 00:36:49.294
They'll also fund think tanks


00:36:49.300 --> 00:36:51.382
that come up with really clever
cultural memes


00:36:54.549 --> 00:36:56.666
and these are things like,
you know,


00:36:56.675 --> 00:36:58.380
"climate science isn't settled",


00:36:58.382 --> 00:37:00.544
which is ripped
straight from tobacco.


00:37:00.550 --> 00:37:02.039
Or "climate change
is a religion".


00:37:02.049 --> 00:37:03.789
<i>That's another one.
It's very evocative.</i>


00:37:03.799 --> 00:37:06.631
They'll also used fake bots
or algorithms


00:37:06.632 --> 00:37:09.169
that appear to be humans
on social media,


00:37:09.174 --> 00:37:11.130
and what this does
is creates a sense


00:37:11.132 --> 00:37:14.044
that they have more support
than they actually do.


00:37:14.049 --> 00:37:17.496
And this one's pretty key
because companies like exxon


00:37:17.507 --> 00:37:20.043
will often fund lots of
different organisations


00:37:20.048 --> 00:37:21.538
to make it look like


00:37:21.548 --> 00:37:23.413
there's this wide consensus
of climate denial,


00:37:23.423 --> 00:37:24.913
whereas in actual fact,


00:37:24.923 --> 00:37:26.879
all those different
organisations


00:37:26.881 --> 00:37:29.418
were funded by the same source.


00:37:33.048 --> 00:37:36.289
These tactics have focused
the conversation


00:37:36.297 --> 00:37:38.914
primarily on our emissions,


00:37:38.923 --> 00:37:42.540
while obscuring a crucial
component of our dilemma.


00:37:44.755 --> 00:37:47.417
If we ceased
all emissions today,


00:37:47.422 --> 00:37:49.287
if we cut emissions to zero,


00:37:49.297 --> 00:37:50.912
human emissions to zero,


00:37:50.922 --> 00:37:52.502
we would still be toast.


00:37:52.504 --> 00:37:54.871
We're still already over
a tipping point


00:37:54.880 --> 00:37:57.541
and on our way
to a point of no return.


00:37:57.546 --> 00:37:59.537
So we have to reduce
the emissions


00:37:59.546 --> 00:38:01.628
that we're producing today,


00:38:01.629 --> 00:38:04.291
and we also have to
sequester carbon


00:38:04.296 --> 00:38:07.128
and we have to remove excess
carbon from the atmosphere


00:38:07.129 --> 00:38:08.618
and store it.


00:38:14.045 --> 00:38:16.127
With an increased sense
of urgency,


00:38:16.128 --> 00:38:18.870
I met with Paul hawken,


00:38:18.878 --> 00:38:21.163
who thankfully
isn't afraid of heights.


00:38:22.795 --> 00:38:25.866
He's the founder of
project drawdown -


00:38:25.877 --> 00:38:29.916
the first comprehensive plan
to reverse global warming.


00:38:31.544 --> 00:38:35.492
If you look at the solutions
that we model in drawdown,


00:38:35.502 --> 00:38:37.992
they're virtually all
regenerative development.


00:38:38.001 --> 00:38:40.664
That is to say
the earth is better off,


00:38:40.669 --> 00:38:43.284
the people are better off,
the communities are better off,


00:38:43.293 --> 00:38:45.159
the creatures are better off,
the birds are better off.


00:38:45.169 --> 00:38:47.875
No matter what it is,
they're better off for it


00:38:47.876 --> 00:38:49.538
than had we not done it.


00:38:52.126 --> 00:38:55.994
Paul suggested I first explore
food and agriculture


00:38:56.001 --> 00:38:58.662
for drawdown opportunities.


00:38:58.667 --> 00:39:01.910
When you change agriculture
practices related to food,


00:39:01.918 --> 00:39:03.407
you can do two things.


00:39:03.417 --> 00:39:06.864
One is you stop emitting
carbon, co2,


00:39:06.875 --> 00:39:10.038
but you're also
sequestering carbon.


00:39:10.042 --> 00:39:12.408
<i>So it's one of those sectors...
Yeah!</i>


00:39:12.417 --> 00:39:14.624
That actually does both -
it's a twofer.


00:39:14.625 --> 00:39:16.616
<i>Which is... not only does it
stop putting it up,</i>


00:39:16.625 --> 00:39:18.115
but it actually brings it down.


00:39:18.125 --> 00:39:20.740
So you're flipping
a whole sector.


00:39:22.292 --> 00:39:25.658
Yeah, I think that people
should be eating


00:39:25.666 --> 00:39:28.783
more fruit and vegetable
and less sweet stuff


00:39:28.791 --> 00:39:33.034
and so that the sweet
stuff should be, um...


00:39:33.041 --> 00:39:35.532
Like, you should be rationed
on sweet stuff.


00:39:35.541 --> 00:39:37.030
When I grow up,


00:39:37.040 --> 00:39:40.908
I want it to be national
hot dog day every day.


00:39:40.915 --> 00:39:44.407
I want to see more trees


00:39:44.415 --> 00:39:49.739
and also want to see chocolate
raining from the clouds.


00:39:51.415 --> 00:39:53.530
Be more healthy.


00:39:53.539 --> 00:39:56.531
That's all I really have to
say about the food.


00:39:56.539 --> 00:39:59.906
Like, I wanted not so much
people eating meat


00:39:59.914 --> 00:40:01.529
because that's animals.


00:40:01.539 --> 00:40:03.029
But I do like bacon.


00:40:03.039 --> 00:40:04.495
Bacon's nice.


00:40:05.664 --> 00:40:07.154
But...


00:40:07.164 --> 00:40:10.860
It still, like, pigs
and I have a pig toy.


00:40:10.871 --> 00:40:14.034
And when I eat bacon,
I feel kind of sad.


00:40:25.870 --> 00:40:28.533
Soil's almost
an unknown universe


00:40:28.538 --> 00:40:32.405
in that there's
over 6 billion microorganisms


00:40:32.412 --> 00:40:34.243
of huge diversity


00:40:34.245 --> 00:40:37.533
in a small spoonful of soil,
of healthy soil.


00:40:37.537 --> 00:40:39.653
Which is really quite amazing.


00:40:39.662 --> 00:40:42.028
Don't know who counted them,
but someone did.


00:40:42.037 --> 00:40:44.528
It's a tough job. Yeah.


00:40:48.786 --> 00:40:54.110
I was in regional Victoria
in Australia to meet col seis,


00:40:54.119 --> 00:40:56.531
a champion of
regenerative agriculture.


00:40:58.161 --> 00:41:01.028
In 1979, we had a major
bushfire at home.


00:41:01.036 --> 00:41:03.151
Like... a wildfire.


00:41:03.160 --> 00:41:05.777
And lost virtually
all of our sheep,


00:41:05.786 --> 00:41:07.901
which were 3,000 merino sheep.


00:41:07.910 --> 00:41:12.529
I went from going ok
to being broke overnight.


00:41:12.535 --> 00:41:14.025
So I had to work out
a way of doing it


00:41:14.035 --> 00:41:15.524
without spending any money.


00:41:15.534 --> 00:41:18.402
And I developed
a different way of farming,


00:41:18.410 --> 00:41:22.527
totally different,
through 1980s and 1990s,


00:41:22.535 --> 00:41:25.276
which turned out to
regenerate the land.


00:41:25.284 --> 00:41:27.617
Using a lot less
chemicals and inputs


00:41:27.618 --> 00:41:29.278
than is traditionally used.


00:41:29.284 --> 00:41:31.115
<i>That's right.
And since I changed</i>


00:41:31.117 --> 00:41:32.526
I actually did the figures
on it 


00:41:32.533 --> 00:41:36.981
I've saved over $2 million,
since I changed.


00:41:36.991 --> 00:41:38.732
I don't go where
the $2 million went.


00:41:43.534 --> 00:41:46.490
Col helps farmers around
the world use plants


00:41:46.491 --> 00:41:48.776
to pull carbon from
the atmosphere


00:41:48.782 --> 00:41:50.273
and put it into their soil...


00:41:51.908 --> 00:41:54.239
Because our constant
ploughing of the soil


00:41:54.240 --> 00:41:56.482
has released billions of
tonnes of the stuff.


00:41:58.532 --> 00:42:01.899
Over the last
10,000 years of agriculture,


00:42:01.907 --> 00:42:03.488
the degradation of soils


00:42:03.490 --> 00:42:07.483
has been one of the leading
causes of climate change.


00:42:07.490 --> 00:42:09.525
It's actually, as of right now,


00:42:09.531 --> 00:42:11.488
a larger cause of climate change


00:42:11.490 --> 00:42:13.401
than burning fossil fuels.


00:42:17.906 --> 00:42:21.273
I asked col to strike
a farming calendar pose


00:42:21.281 --> 00:42:23.488
to explain how he brings
the carbon back home.


00:42:26.530 --> 00:42:29.898
Plants use carbon dioxide
and energy from the sun


00:42:29.906 --> 00:42:31.736
to create simple sugars.


00:42:33.531 --> 00:42:36.112
The plant uses
some of these sugars to grow.


00:42:36.113 --> 00:42:39.025
The rest is pumped into
the soil, through the roots.


00:42:40.155 --> 00:42:42.737
These sugars feed soil microbes,


00:42:42.738 --> 00:42:44.854
which interact with the plant


00:42:44.863 --> 00:42:47.854
and the carbon dioxide
taken from the atmosphere


00:42:47.862 --> 00:42:50.730
is sequestered into the soil
as carbon.


00:42:57.904 --> 00:42:59.393
- G'day!
- Fraser.


00:42:59.403 --> 00:43:00.894
- Hey, Damon.
- Nice to meet you, mate.


00:43:00.904 --> 00:43:02.394
- You too.
- How you going?


00:43:02.404 --> 00:43:04.610
- Very well. G'day. Col.
- G'day.


00:43:07.154 --> 00:43:09.485
We went to
take the kids fishing


00:43:09.486 --> 00:43:12.603
and we had to go and find worms
on the farm


00:43:12.611 --> 00:43:16.102
and Fraser was just going from
paddock to paddock


00:43:16.110 --> 00:43:17.647
trying to find worms.


00:43:17.653 --> 00:43:19.393
- There wasn't any.
- There was none.


00:43:19.403 --> 00:43:20.892
And it ended up...


00:43:20.902 --> 00:43:22.768
This fun experience
ended up being not so fun


00:43:22.778 --> 00:43:24.268
because there was
so much frustration


00:43:24.278 --> 00:43:26.984
about how come there weren't
any worms and...


00:43:26.985 --> 00:43:28.725
'Cause healthy soil has worms.


00:43:30.485 --> 00:43:32.726
We had everything
going for our farm


00:43:32.735 --> 00:43:35.147
and I didn't feel that
it was getting the yields


00:43:35.152 --> 00:43:36.857
and getting the results that,
you know,


00:43:36.859 --> 00:43:38.521
I thought were going
to be there.


00:43:38.527 --> 00:43:41.893
And I tried just throwing
more fertiliser at it


00:43:41.901 --> 00:43:43.483
and that didn't seem to work.


00:43:43.485 --> 00:43:45.145
And so I just had
all these questions


00:43:45.151 --> 00:43:49.020
around what we were doing
and why we were doing it.


00:43:51.734 --> 00:43:53.473
So, what's going on here,
fellas?


00:43:53.483 --> 00:43:56.976
So, this is what we call
a chemical summer fallow.


00:43:56.984 --> 00:43:58.018
Yep.


00:43:58.025 --> 00:43:59.515
Where we control
the weeds


00:43:59.525 --> 00:44:00.982
with a chemical over summer.


00:44:00.984 --> 00:44:02.473
So, how does that
affect the soil,


00:44:02.483 --> 00:44:04.394
like, in terms of the...


00:44:04.400 --> 00:44:06.140
This form of agriculture


00:44:06.150 --> 00:44:08.607
really kills
the whole soil ecosystem.


00:44:10.775 --> 00:44:13.016
So, what are we
looking at there, col?


00:44:13.025 --> 00:44:16.222
That hard compaction,
poor-structured soil


00:44:16.233 --> 00:44:19.019
is really not good
in agriculture.


00:44:19.024 --> 00:44:21.356
Like, if you think about a plant


00:44:21.357 --> 00:44:24.895
trying to get through that,
it's almost impossible. Um...


00:44:24.899 --> 00:44:27.731
The other thing too,
water can't get in there.


00:44:27.732 --> 00:44:30.018
To counteract
these problems,


00:44:30.024 --> 00:44:34.017
col's simple advice to Fraser
was to start planting.


00:44:36.523 --> 00:44:38.014
Ok, so what we've done here


00:44:38.024 --> 00:44:40.639
is planted a mix of
different species.


00:44:40.648 --> 00:44:42.138
About 80 in total.


00:44:43.231 --> 00:44:46.394
They all play a role
in the soil health,


00:44:46.398 --> 00:44:48.137
with their different roots


00:44:48.147 --> 00:44:50.605
and their different
root exudates.


00:44:50.606 --> 00:44:55.521
So we've got the sunflower here
and the sorghum.


00:44:55.523 --> 00:44:58.138
- Um, millet.
- Yeah, right.


00:44:58.147 --> 00:44:59.512
Down here.


00:44:59.522 --> 00:45:01.854
And all these together
means with that variety,


00:45:01.855 --> 00:45:03.345
it's making the soil...


00:45:03.355 --> 00:45:04.844
It's pulling the carbon
into the soil


00:45:04.854 --> 00:45:06.095
and making it healthier.


00:45:06.105 --> 00:45:07.766
That's right. Yeah. Exactly.


00:45:07.772 --> 00:45:09.636
- Go, nature!
- Yeah.


00:45:20.853 --> 00:45:24.392
If we go back even
a few thousand years,


00:45:24.396 --> 00:45:26.101
all of the grasslands
in the world


00:45:26.103 --> 00:45:29.641
were dominated by large mobs of
grazing animals.


00:45:32.145 --> 00:45:37.343
And also, they were kept moving
all the time by predators.


00:45:37.352 --> 00:45:39.139
So the animals -
in this case, cattle -


00:45:39.145 --> 00:45:41.601
are never kept on
the same area at one time.


00:45:43.770 --> 00:45:46.385
By letting the cattle
run amok in the crops,


00:45:46.394 --> 00:45:48.350
good things happen.


00:45:49.395 --> 00:45:52.261
They flatten the field,


00:45:52.269 --> 00:45:55.341
they create a utopia
for the local insects


00:45:55.352 --> 00:45:57.513
which becomes
a natural fertiliser,


00:45:57.519 --> 00:46:02.387
and crucially, they are fed
as nature intended.


00:46:05.519 --> 00:46:07.133
Over the last
50 or 60 years,


00:46:07.143 --> 00:46:10.511
we've removed animals
like cattle and sheep


00:46:10.519 --> 00:46:12.509
from eating their natural food


00:46:12.518 --> 00:46:15.224
and put them in feedlots,
eating grain.


00:46:15.225 --> 00:46:16.636
And we should
never have done that


00:46:16.643 --> 00:46:18.725
because they have never
evolved to eat grain.


00:46:20.142 --> 00:46:23.009
So what happens is that
the cattle are basically sick.


00:46:24.100 --> 00:46:27.467
And we are finding out now
that the meat from feedlots


00:46:27.475 --> 00:46:29.011
is not healthy for us.


00:46:29.017 --> 00:46:31.382
It's certainly not healthy
for the animals.


00:46:31.391 --> 00:46:34.384
And it's something that
we should never have done.


00:46:37.767 --> 00:46:39.848
Any discussion
around livestock


00:46:39.849 --> 00:46:42.261
has to include methane,


00:46:42.266 --> 00:46:45.508
which is another damaging
greenhouse gas,


00:46:45.516 --> 00:46:48.713
quite rudely burped out
by the cows.


00:46:55.016 --> 00:46:58.382
While some livestock practices
offset that damage,


00:46:58.390 --> 00:47:01.222
the bad news for the hot dog kid


00:47:01.223 --> 00:47:04.260
is that we need to embrace
a more plant-rich diet.


00:47:05.723 --> 00:47:07.133
And the meat we do eat


00:47:07.140 --> 00:47:10.006
should ideally come from
regenerative practices


00:47:10.014 --> 00:47:11.505
like Fraser's.


00:47:12.889 --> 00:47:15.346
Plant-rich diet means that
we actually reduce


00:47:15.347 --> 00:47:19.841
the amount of protein that
we eat in wealthier nations,


00:47:19.847 --> 00:47:21.383
because it's too high.


00:47:21.389 --> 00:47:23.755
It's 100-110g per day.


00:47:23.764 --> 00:47:27.837
What we model is
decreasing that to 50-60 g.


00:47:29.097 --> 00:47:31.337
The fact is, some people
want to eat meat.


00:47:31.346 --> 00:47:34.133
What meat they eat
is critically important.


00:47:34.138 --> 00:47:36.845
How it's produced,
how it relates to the land


00:47:36.846 --> 00:47:39.007
are very, very important.


00:47:46.512 --> 00:47:50.256
But as our climate becomes
increasingly volatile,


00:47:50.263 --> 00:47:53.709
this type of farming has
one more crucial advantage.


00:47:55.470 --> 00:48:00.089
For every 1% increase in carbon
to 30cm,


00:48:00.095 --> 00:48:03.507
we increase the water holding
capacity of soil


00:48:03.511 --> 00:48:06.709
by 166,000 litres per hectare.


00:48:06.720 --> 00:48:08.129
On every rainfall event.


00:48:11.511 --> 00:48:14.002
When it rains,
the soil on the left,


00:48:14.011 --> 00:48:16.878
with more carbon
and organic matter,


00:48:16.886 --> 00:48:20.127
absorbs the water
and less runs off the top.


00:48:21.886 --> 00:48:24.376
Whereas the chemical-ridden
soil on the right


00:48:24.385 --> 00:48:29.379
absorbs almost no water,
and allows it to run off,


00:48:29.385 --> 00:48:31.376
taking the chemicals with it


00:48:31.385 --> 00:48:33.751
into nearby rivers
and waterways.


00:48:36.592 --> 00:48:38.379
That's the way we can
buffer against droughts


00:48:38.385 --> 00:48:39.875
and dry seasons.


00:48:39.885 --> 00:48:41.374
Grasslands,
all around the world,


00:48:41.384 --> 00:48:44.092
they virtually had a built-in
irrigation system.


00:48:45.509 --> 00:48:48.125
Really can see
the aggregation that's going on


00:48:48.134 --> 00:48:49.624
from all these roots


00:48:49.634 --> 00:48:51.875
of all the different plants
we've been growing in here.


00:48:51.884 --> 00:48:53.966
So what we've got going here
with this clod


00:48:53.967 --> 00:48:56.128
is basically a dry paddock


00:48:56.134 --> 00:48:59.877
with, um, not much growing
in it, apart from a few weeds.


00:48:59.884 --> 00:49:01.589
And here, three months ago,


00:49:01.591 --> 00:49:06.335
we planted the multi-species
grazing crop, yeah.


00:49:07.508 --> 00:49:09.589
So that's in three
months that's happened?


00:49:09.590 --> 00:49:11.752
<i>That's in three-months.
It's incredible, isn't it?</i>


00:49:11.758 --> 00:49:14.749
That kind of represents big ag,


00:49:14.757 --> 00:49:17.249
and this represents
mother nature.


00:49:17.258 --> 00:49:19.623
And it's pretty obvious
who I'd rather


00:49:19.632 --> 00:49:21.748
get my source of vegetables
from.


00:49:21.757 --> 00:49:24.999
Well, when we talk
about trying to work out


00:49:25.007 --> 00:49:27.874
some of the global problems
and trying to do it quickly,


00:49:27.882 --> 00:49:30.588
well, you can really see the
speed that it can happen here.


00:49:30.589 --> 00:49:31.999
I mean, look at all these roots.


00:49:32.006 --> 00:49:34.463
Like, that's all carbon pumped,
isn't it?


00:49:34.464 --> 00:49:38.253
<i>It is. It really shows that
plants, plants and more plants.</i>


00:49:38.257 --> 00:49:41.123
In other words,
plant diversity, um,


00:49:41.131 --> 00:49:42.996
plus grazing animals


00:49:43.006 --> 00:49:45.497
will create that
and fix our problems.


00:49:47.380 --> 00:49:51.248
People ask me, "well,
what can we say to farmers?"


00:49:51.255 --> 00:49:53.872
And the answer is,
"we can't do without you."


00:49:53.881 --> 00:49:56.712
We can't mitigate climate
change without agriculture,


00:49:56.713 --> 00:49:58.624
so we have to find ways,


00:50:02.130 --> 00:50:04.085
instead of part of the problem.


00:50:07.130 --> 00:50:09.085
Fraser will now
let his multi-species crop


00:50:09.087 --> 00:50:10.749
break down.


00:50:12.754 --> 00:50:15.245
He can then plant
his commercial crops here


00:50:15.254 --> 00:50:18.246
without having to break
the soil and release carbon.


00:50:20.003 --> 00:50:22.245
But perhaps most importantly,


00:50:22.254 --> 00:50:24.619
he can now
take the kids fishing.


00:50:36.003 --> 00:50:38.870
So, I just bake it for
20 to 30 minutes?


00:50:38.878 --> 00:50:40.833
Yes, that's it. You've got it.


00:50:40.835 --> 00:50:42.325
And then, once it's done,


00:50:42.335 --> 00:50:44.452
you just sprinkle the Greens
on the top and that's it.


00:50:44.461 --> 00:50:45.700
Beautiful.


00:50:45.710 --> 00:50:47.996
- Thanks, nan. Love you.
- Ok. Bye, darl.


00:50:48.002 --> 00:50:49.367
Bye!


00:50:49.377 --> 00:50:52.209
By 2040, my hope, velvet,


00:50:52.210 --> 00:50:54.496
is that more people
will have an understanding


00:50:54.502 --> 00:50:57.868
of how the foods they eat
impact our environment.


00:51:00.210 --> 00:51:02.200
If this transpires,


00:51:02.209 --> 00:51:04.872
your generation are likely
to eat a lot less meat


00:51:04.877 --> 00:51:06.366
than mine did.


00:51:09.334 --> 00:51:11.120
There's no doubt
we'll continue our obsession


00:51:11.126 --> 00:51:12.491
with fad diets.


00:51:12.501 --> 00:51:15.242
We find it's the flavour-free
diet that's the way to go


00:51:15.250 --> 00:51:16.707
because let's face it 


00:51:16.709 --> 00:51:18.198
it's the flavour
that gets us into trouble.


00:51:18.208 --> 00:51:19.994
Isn't that right?


00:51:20.000 --> 00:51:21.992
I'll just love
tending our garden.


00:51:23.000 --> 00:51:24.490
From an app.


00:51:26.000 --> 00:51:28.582
And your mum and I will
completely understand


00:51:28.583 --> 00:51:30.868
when you start avoiding
dinner at our house.


00:51:36.124 --> 00:51:38.366
With less global
consumption of meat,


00:51:38.375 --> 00:51:41.366
aided by some pretty convincing
substitutes...


00:51:44.332 --> 00:51:47.074
We could see many feedlots
close down.


00:51:49.624 --> 00:51:52.239
And animals returned to the land


00:51:52.248 --> 00:51:54.240
for use in regenerative farming.


00:51:59.123 --> 00:52:01.364
But just imagine
what we could do


00:52:03.997 --> 00:52:07.240
that is currently being used
to grow food for animals.


00:52:11.581 --> 00:52:13.366
This land could transition


00:52:13.372 --> 00:52:16.329
to a range of
regenerative practices


00:52:16.330 --> 00:52:20.743
that drawdown vast amounts
of carbon into the soil,


00:52:20.746 --> 00:52:23.238
while retaining precious water


00:52:23.247 --> 00:52:25.737
and producing
nutrient-dense food.


00:52:31.204 --> 00:52:36.198
Instead of soy or grains, we
could feed the animals grass,


00:52:36.204 --> 00:52:39.992
crop residue, or food waste.


00:52:40.996 --> 00:52:43.487
This would improve
the health of the animals,


00:52:43.496 --> 00:52:46.862
the people who choose to
eat them


00:52:46.870 --> 00:52:48.736
and our environment.


00:52:55.078 --> 00:52:57.819
The steep croplands
around the world, darling,


00:52:57.827 --> 00:52:59.693
could be used to grow foods


00:52:59.703 --> 00:53:02.444
that also draw down huge
amounts of carbon.


00:53:04.578 --> 00:53:07.990
A practice called agroforestry
does this


00:53:07.994 --> 00:53:10.861
by simultaneously growing foods
like pawpaw,


00:53:10.869 --> 00:53:15.362
bananas, coffee, avocados
or vegetables,


00:53:15.368 --> 00:53:18.860
but crucially,
on small parcels of land.


00:53:21.494 --> 00:53:24.860
With highly diversified
local food systems,


00:53:24.868 --> 00:53:30.067
you have a way of really
dramatically decreasing


00:53:30.076 --> 00:53:34.866
the scale of land, water,
energy needed,


00:53:34.868 --> 00:53:36.823
and massively increasing


00:53:36.825 --> 00:53:38.737
how much you can get
from that land.


00:53:45.742 --> 00:53:48.233
Industrial ag says,
"without us, you're not...


00:53:48.242 --> 00:53:49.982
<i>"You're going to starve.
You're not going to eat."</i>


00:53:49.992 --> 00:53:52.858
Well, actually between 70% and
80% of the food in the world


00:53:52.866 --> 00:53:54.732
is created by small holders.


00:53:58.866 --> 00:54:03.439
Industrial ag provides
20% of the food in the world.


00:54:03.449 --> 00:54:07.613
Most of that is corn
and soy fed to animals.


00:54:07.616 --> 00:54:10.483
A lot of that is sugar,
which we don't need.


00:54:11.741 --> 00:54:15.107
So the idea that big ag
is going to save us,


00:54:15.115 --> 00:54:17.857
or without which we cannot exist


00:54:17.865 --> 00:54:19.605
is absolutely upside down
and backwards,


00:54:19.615 --> 00:54:21.355
because they produce sickness,


00:54:21.365 --> 00:54:23.731
they produce obesity,
they produce diabetes.


00:54:23.740 --> 00:54:25.354
That's what big ag is producing.


00:54:28.490 --> 00:54:30.070
I hope one day, velvet,


00:54:30.072 --> 00:54:33.485
you get to read the story of
how we reached this future.


00:54:36.697 --> 00:54:38.688
It's about how those
who polluted our air


00:54:38.697 --> 00:54:42.232
with excess carbon
had to pay a penalty.


00:54:43.822 --> 00:54:45.312
And the money raised


00:54:45.322 --> 00:54:48.109
paid farmers to clean our air


00:54:48.114 --> 00:54:49.729
by putting it back
into the soil.


00:54:53.446 --> 00:54:55.232
This helped restore
the climate...


00:54:56.988 --> 00:54:58.693
Biodiversity loss...


00:54:59.988 --> 00:55:01.478
And land conversion...


00:55:02.487 --> 00:55:04.945
While pulling more people
into the doughnut


00:55:04.946 --> 00:55:07.857
by saving precious water,


00:55:07.862 --> 00:55:10.979
improving the quality
of our food,


00:55:10.987 --> 00:55:14.184
and in turn
improving our health.


00:55:27.486 --> 00:55:29.067
It's interesting,
making this film,


00:55:29.069 --> 00:55:30.980
the more that
I'm learning about...


00:55:32.486 --> 00:55:35.727
You realise how hard it is
to actually do the right thing.


00:55:35.735 --> 00:55:39.684
And in a sense, it's tempting
to kind of just shut down


00:55:39.694 --> 00:55:41.183
and switch off


00:55:41.193 --> 00:55:43.480
because, I mean, here I am
sitting on an aeroplane


00:55:43.486 --> 00:55:46.727
that is spewing out
half a tonne of carbon,


00:55:46.735 --> 00:55:48.350
making this film,


00:55:48.360 --> 00:55:50.225
or getting water in this cup


00:55:50.235 --> 00:55:51.975
that's probably going to go
straight into the ocean


00:55:51.985 --> 00:55:53.691
and hurt a baby turtle,


00:55:53.693 --> 00:55:55.684
and I don't want to know that.


00:55:55.693 --> 00:55:57.182
And most people don't want to
know about that,


00:55:57.192 --> 00:55:58.682
so they switch off.


00:55:58.692 --> 00:56:02.355
And you can't help but be
a hypocrite at the moment


00:56:02.359 --> 00:56:06.352
because our entire system is
built on and by fossil fuels.


00:56:07.442 --> 00:56:08.932
Fossil fuels have done us
really well


00:56:08.942 --> 00:56:10.307
to get to this point.


00:56:10.317 --> 00:56:11.807
There's some great
things in the world.


00:56:11.817 --> 00:56:13.306
But we can't keep doing
what we're doing.


00:56:13.316 --> 00:56:14.681
It's actually impossible


00:56:14.691 --> 00:56:16.808
to maintain the resources
we're using.


00:56:16.817 --> 00:56:18.807
So we're going to
have to transition,


00:56:18.816 --> 00:56:20.306
and it's going to be
a little bit awkward,


00:56:20.316 --> 00:56:22.307
if we're going to give
our kids a better future.


00:56:38.482 --> 00:56:40.347
# to the place on the map #


00:56:40.357 --> 00:56:43.724
# from which no-one
has ever returned... #


00:56:44.982 --> 00:56:47.313
While storing carbon
in our soil


00:56:47.314 --> 00:56:50.727
will go a long way
to solving our problem,


00:56:50.731 --> 00:56:54.304
there is a limit to how much
carbon soil can hold.


00:56:55.439 --> 00:56:58.306
So we're going to need another
leg-up from mother nature.


00:57:00.564 --> 00:57:02.975
Make sure coral looks ok


00:57:02.980 --> 00:57:05.973
'cause it normally looks like
a rainbow in the sea


00:57:05.981 --> 00:57:08.437
with all the fishes,


00:57:08.438 --> 00:57:10.680
but sometimes if you litter,


00:57:10.689 --> 00:57:13.430
it looks a bit not good.


00:57:13.438 --> 00:57:18.478
<i>It looks like... a big, big
mess.</i>


00:57:19.687 --> 00:57:21.974
At the beach,
it would just not be the same


00:57:21.980 --> 00:57:23.970
if you can't swim in the water.


00:57:23.979 --> 00:57:26.972
And also all the really amazing
marine animals


00:57:26.980 --> 00:57:29.846
like vaquita, whales, sea fish 


00:57:29.854 --> 00:57:32.971
if they were all gone, then
it just wouldn't be the same,


00:57:32.979 --> 00:57:34.219
the world.


00:57:34.229 --> 00:57:37.801
I want the oceans to be cleaner.


00:57:41.228 --> 00:57:42.719
One of the coming
attractions


00:57:42.729 --> 00:57:44.969
that I think is gonna to have
the biggest impact


00:57:44.978 --> 00:57:48.721
is what is called
marine permaculture.


00:57:48.728 --> 00:57:51.185
It's just another way of
farming in the ocean,


00:57:51.186 --> 00:57:53.677
but this is a more wild farming


00:57:53.686 --> 00:57:55.847
because it actually
sets up the context


00:57:55.853 --> 00:57:58.811
for the oceans to
regenerate themselves.


00:58:08.352 --> 00:58:11.059
Dr Brian Von herzen,
a physicist,


00:58:11.060 --> 00:58:12.550
used to fly to Europe every year


00:58:12.560 --> 00:58:16.303
and he saw how quickly the
Greenland ice sheet was melting


00:58:16.310 --> 00:58:18.971
and decided to change
his whole life in 2006


00:58:18.976 --> 00:58:20.466
to restoring living systems,


00:58:20.476 --> 00:58:21.966
and focused on the ocean.


00:58:26.226 --> 00:58:28.592
Brian told me
to pack a lunch...


00:58:28.601 --> 00:58:30.556
And a sick bag,


00:58:30.558 --> 00:58:33.096
as we were spending the day
out at sea.


00:58:39.683 --> 00:58:41.594
With 93% of global warming


00:58:41.600 --> 00:58:43.591
going into the ocean today
each year,


00:58:43.600 --> 00:58:46.557
the problem is that
the waters are getting too warm


00:58:46.558 --> 00:58:48.970
and without enough
overturning circulation,


00:58:48.975 --> 00:58:50.556
and there aren't enough
nutrients,


00:58:50.558 --> 00:58:53.470
and the result is
we lose the life in the ocean.


00:58:55.182 --> 00:58:57.969
Understandably,
there has been reluctance


00:58:57.974 --> 00:58:59.714
to meddle with our oceans.


00:59:00.974 --> 00:59:02.714
But the fact that they
are getting warmer,


00:59:02.724 --> 00:59:04.806
becoming acidic,


00:59:04.807 --> 00:59:08.344
and many fish species are now
in danger of collapse...


00:59:10.182 --> 00:59:12.342
Means that they
actually need our help.


00:59:15.474 --> 00:59:18.089
Brian's solution is seaweed.


00:59:19.973 --> 00:59:22.464
So what we do
with marine permaculture


00:59:22.473 --> 00:59:25.669
is we restore
overturning circulation.


00:59:25.680 --> 00:59:28.797
It's as if your leg was asleep
and you lost circulation.


00:59:28.805 --> 00:59:30.466
We have to do that
with the ocean.


00:59:30.472 --> 00:59:32.963
We've gotta get that
overturning circulation


00:59:32.972 --> 00:59:33.961
going again


00:59:33.972 --> 00:59:36.929
to bring the cool waters up
with the higher nutrient levels


00:59:36.930 --> 00:59:40.218
that can actually restore
conditions for the seaweeds.


00:59:41.971 --> 00:59:43.963
Between Australia
and the United States,


00:59:43.972 --> 00:59:47.589
<i>there's 100,000,000 km?
Of ocean desert</i>


00:59:47.596 --> 00:59:50.463
that is amenable to
marine permaculture.


00:59:51.554 --> 00:59:54.842
We can actually restore life
in subtropical oceans,


00:59:54.846 --> 00:59:58.839
restore the fish habitat that's
needed to restore fisheries.


01:00:01.054 --> 01:00:04.672
Marine permaculture
is a regenerative technology


01:00:04.679 --> 01:00:06.168
that works like this.


01:00:07.428 --> 01:00:10.044
A frame made of
recycled material


01:00:10.053 --> 01:00:13.045
becomes a platform for
the seaweed to grow on.


01:00:13.053 --> 01:00:15.294
It sits just below the surface


01:00:15.303 --> 01:00:18.794
and sinks lower as the
seaweed grows and gets heavier.


01:00:21.178 --> 01:00:24.715
A pump powered simply by
the rise and fall of the ocean,


01:00:24.719 --> 01:00:27.836
brings the cold, nutrient-laden
waters from below


01:00:27.844 --> 01:00:30.426
and disperses them
over the seaweed


01:00:30.427 --> 01:00:32.588
in the upper warmer layer.


01:00:32.594 --> 01:00:34.959
The seaweed can be
regularly harvested


01:00:34.968 --> 01:00:37.051
and used for a range
of purposes.


01:00:38.427 --> 01:00:42.340
Seaweed is good for food,
animal feed, fertiliser,


01:00:42.344 --> 01:00:43.924
fibre and biofuel.


01:00:43.926 --> 01:00:45.962
There's so much we can do
with seaweeds.


01:00:53.593 --> 01:00:59.213
So, we're cruising along in
this very low-carbon vessel,


01:00:59.218 --> 01:01:00.957
following Brian,


01:01:00.967 --> 01:01:03.458
who's swum down
to get the seaweed


01:01:03.467 --> 01:01:05.833
in one of his testing sites
here.


01:01:10.717 --> 01:01:12.298
Except he's gone,


01:01:12.300 --> 01:01:15.542
and he's been underwater for
about two minutes and, um...


01:01:15.550 --> 01:01:16.834
Brian!


01:01:18.841 --> 01:01:20.706
Brian?!


01:01:20.716 --> 01:01:22.547
- I found it.
- You found it.


01:01:22.549 --> 01:01:24.414
- Yeah.
- Alright, we'll come to you.


01:01:26.091 --> 01:01:27.581
The permaculture tests


01:01:27.591 --> 01:01:31.082
that Brian and his team have
conducted in the south pacific


01:01:31.090 --> 01:01:33.672
have seen marine life return,


01:01:33.673 --> 01:01:37.416
and the seaweed transform the
quality of the water itself.


01:01:40.548 --> 01:01:42.834
Seaweeds actually
draw down


01:01:42.840 --> 01:01:45.547
carbon dioxide
from the ocean waters.


01:01:46.922 --> 01:01:49.539
And they restore the alkalinity
of the ocean.


01:01:50.839 --> 01:01:54.582
That enables shellfish
and other creatures to thrive.


01:01:58.589 --> 01:02:01.331
Damon, I thought we'd try
some seaweed salad today.


01:02:01.339 --> 01:02:02.828
Oh.


01:02:05.839 --> 01:02:07.669
Well, the little bursts of gas
are good.


01:02:07.671 --> 01:02:09.161
Yeah. Like little poppers.


01:02:12.213 --> 01:02:14.204
Ok. That's actually pretty good.


01:02:14.213 --> 01:02:15.794
Seaweed salad.


01:02:15.796 --> 01:02:18.333
<i>So, what kind of... we're
getting omega 3s in here.</i>


01:02:18.338 --> 01:02:19.577
- What kind of...
- Oh, yeah.


01:02:19.587 --> 01:02:20.919
What's our source?


01:02:20.921 --> 01:02:23.707
Epa, dha, antioxidants,
phytonutrients,


01:02:23.712 --> 01:02:25.327
omega nutrients.


01:02:26.671 --> 01:02:31.414
But this humble weed has
one more heroic trait to offer.


01:02:32.587 --> 01:02:36.078
It could play a profound role
in drawing down carbon.


01:02:37.295 --> 01:02:39.206
Well, this is an example
of brown seaweed.


01:02:39.212 --> 01:02:43.330
The fastest brown seaweeds
will grow half a metre per day.


01:02:43.337 --> 01:02:45.577
Hang on. Half a metre a day?


01:02:45.586 --> 01:02:47.076
Half a metre a day.


01:02:47.086 --> 01:02:48.951
And they'll grow over 50m long.


01:02:48.961 --> 01:02:51.452
Is that one of the fastest
growing plants in the world?


01:02:51.461 --> 01:02:54.328
It is the fastest-growing
tree on the planet.


01:02:54.336 --> 01:02:56.576
<i>Wow. Ok. So that's why
the understanding of...</i>


01:02:56.585 --> 01:02:58.668
That it's going to be pulling
carbon out of the atmosphere


01:02:58.669 --> 01:03:00.659
very quickly,
because it grows so fast.


01:03:00.668 --> 01:03:05.037
Thousands of tonnes of carbon
per square kilometre per year.


01:03:06.460 --> 01:03:07.825
Wow. Ok.


01:03:09.084 --> 01:03:10.916
Win! It's not a weed at all.


01:03:10.918 --> 01:03:13.954
It's food,
it's habitat for fish,


01:03:13.959 --> 01:03:16.042
it gives back to
the fish ecosystems,


01:03:16.043 --> 01:03:17.532
and we balance carbon.


01:03:17.542 --> 01:03:19.078
We measure the carbon export.


01:03:21.834 --> 01:03:24.666
The testing of large-scale
marine permaculture


01:03:24.667 --> 01:03:26.077
is yet to be done.


01:03:27.542 --> 01:03:29.532
But the stakes
couldn't be any higher


01:03:29.541 --> 01:03:31.031
for getting them underway.


01:03:32.834 --> 01:03:38.328
This is all about restoring the
foundation of our civilisation,


01:03:38.333 --> 01:03:40.665
because with climate disruption,


01:03:40.666 --> 01:03:43.328
we'll deplete the soils,
we'll deplete the oceans.


01:03:43.333 --> 01:03:45.449
There will be
no civilisation left


01:03:45.458 --> 01:03:49.076
because any time
you lose that foundation


01:03:49.083 --> 01:03:51.289
of food and the soils
and the seas,


01:03:51.290 --> 01:03:53.031
you lose civilisation as well.


01:04:18.664 --> 01:04:22.828
So, velvet, because
the warming will increase


01:04:22.831 --> 01:04:25.446
over the next two decades,


01:04:25.455 --> 01:04:28.197
and sea levels
will continue to rise...


01:04:29.538 --> 01:04:35.659
Some countries will need to
adapt in new and creative ways.


01:04:42.955 --> 01:04:45.696
But if predictions of severe
droughts are realised


01:04:45.704 --> 01:04:48.821
that see shortages of food,


01:04:48.829 --> 01:04:52.447
the potential for unprecedented
numbers of refugees


01:04:52.454 --> 01:04:54.820
and civil unrest
around the world...


01:04:56.412 --> 01:04:57.901
Is enormous.


01:05:05.411 --> 01:05:09.324
Just imagine large-scale
marine permaculture,


01:05:09.328 --> 01:05:11.944
helping to mitigate
the severity of such a crisis.


01:05:15.078 --> 01:05:16.943
It could provide
an alternative food source


01:05:16.953 --> 01:05:18.442
to the damaged land crops...


01:05:20.286 --> 01:05:22.822
That is rich in protein
and other nutrients.


01:05:26.077 --> 01:05:27.567
Instead of having
governments


01:05:27.577 --> 01:05:29.442
that are reacting to disaster,


01:05:29.452 --> 01:05:31.317
we need governments
and communities


01:05:31.327 --> 01:05:34.523
and businesses that proactively
set a new vision


01:05:34.534 --> 01:05:36.400
that prevents these disasters,


01:05:36.410 --> 01:05:38.449
that actually takes us
off in a different direction.


01:05:41.201 --> 01:05:44.568
Investment in marine
permaculture around the world


01:05:44.576 --> 01:05:47.318
could generate countless jobs


01:05:47.326 --> 01:05:49.942
and help us reduce
our meat consumption,


01:05:49.951 --> 01:05:52.657
while sequestering staggering
amounts of carbon.


01:05:56.575 --> 01:06:01.444
Imagine decommissioned oil rigs
converted to house the workers


01:06:01.450 --> 01:06:04.442
and becoming exciting
tourist destinations


01:06:04.450 --> 01:06:07.283
for those keen
to explore marine life.


01:06:15.824 --> 01:06:18.190
The seaweed could be
particularly valuable


01:06:18.199 --> 01:06:22.943
in climate vulnerable areas
like the bay of bengal,


01:06:22.949 --> 01:06:26.646
where it could provide food,
fertiliser and biofuel.


01:06:28.074 --> 01:06:31.692
Along the coast of Africa, it
could provide similar benefits,


01:06:31.699 --> 01:06:34.405
contributing to a thriving
local economy.


01:06:40.198 --> 01:06:42.940
With mechanisms,
simple ones,


01:06:42.948 --> 01:06:46.314
you are in fact
regenerating the oceans.


01:06:49.280 --> 01:06:50.941
You could feed 10 billion people


01:06:50.947 --> 01:06:53.689
with the protein from
marine permaculture alone.


01:07:13.945 --> 01:07:15.652
At this point of the trip,


01:07:15.654 --> 01:07:19.566
I felt for the first time
like we genuinely have a chance


01:07:19.570 --> 01:07:21.061
at a better future...


01:07:22.154 --> 01:07:23.814
If we choose to take it.


01:07:29.320 --> 01:07:33.438
But before heading home, there
was another subject to address.


01:07:37.570 --> 01:07:40.185
Well, I wish they'd stop
killing off animals


01:07:40.194 --> 01:07:42.686
and, well, forests and whatnot.


01:07:44.277 --> 01:07:45.767
That'd be cool.


01:07:46.777 --> 01:07:50.816
Probably for people to
acknowledge that, like,


01:07:50.819 --> 01:07:52.524
the factories we build


01:07:52.526 --> 01:07:55.939
and, like, they hurt nature
sometimes.


01:07:55.943 --> 01:07:58.060
Like, the things
that we produce,


01:07:58.069 --> 01:08:02.687
it can, like, hurt the wildlife
and it's not good.


01:08:02.693 --> 01:08:04.809
But people just ignore it
right now.


01:08:04.818 --> 01:08:08.685
I would like to see everyone
using the three rs 


01:08:08.692 --> 01:08:11.184
reduce, reuse and recycle.


01:08:11.193 --> 01:08:14.560
Many people know about it,
but don't practice it today.


01:08:26.567 --> 01:08:29.023
Today's economy, right?


01:08:29.024 --> 01:08:30.811
It gets bigger
faster and faster.


01:08:30.817 --> 01:08:34.434
So the economy is doubling
in size nearly every 20 years.


01:08:36.317 --> 01:08:39.638
We take earth's materials,
turn them into stuff we want,


01:08:39.649 --> 01:08:41.856
we use it for a while
and then throw it away.


01:08:41.857 --> 01:08:42.846
Yep.


01:08:42.857 --> 01:08:44.393
And that take, make, use, lose 


01:08:44.399 --> 01:08:46.764
it cuts against the
living cycles of the planet,


01:08:46.773 --> 01:08:49.845
causing climate change,
causing pollution.


01:08:52.899 --> 01:08:55.389
So we're kicking ourselves
out of planetary balance.


01:08:59.231 --> 01:09:01.517
It is difficult to see
the impacts


01:09:01.523 --> 01:09:04.640
of our individual resource use
on the broader environment.


01:09:07.397 --> 01:09:11.094
But our survival could depend
on us making that connection.


01:09:13.647 --> 01:09:17.515
For greater than
99.9% of our evolution,


01:09:17.522 --> 01:09:20.514
human beings lived in this
very intimate association


01:09:20.522 --> 01:09:22.353
with the natural world.


01:09:22.355 --> 01:09:26.098
The decisions we made
on a daily basis as individuals


01:09:26.105 --> 01:09:28.265
within the context of
communities,


01:09:28.271 --> 01:09:30.603
they were informed by
this immediate feedback


01:09:30.604 --> 01:09:32.890
we got from the world around us.


01:09:32.896 --> 01:09:36.388
From the sun, from the water
that was falling on us.


01:09:36.396 --> 01:09:38.762
And you fast forward
to this little blip of time


01:09:38.771 --> 01:09:41.386
that we're in right now,
and, you know,


01:09:41.395 --> 01:09:43.636
we're completely and totally
alienated from nature.


01:09:43.645 --> 01:09:45.135
We've lost that connection.


01:09:48.770 --> 01:09:51.637
I've come to the town of
oberlin in Ohio,


01:09:53.270 --> 01:09:55.636
have come up with
a quite brilliant way


01:09:55.645 --> 01:09:59.092
to raise awareness of
their community's resource use.


01:10:02.102 --> 01:10:03.843
The environmental dashboard


01:10:03.853 --> 01:10:05.763
was really kind of built around
the science


01:10:05.769 --> 01:10:07.885
and putting people and humans
in connection with


01:10:07.894 --> 01:10:10.010
how they're using
their natural resources.


01:10:10.019 --> 01:10:11.759
And so what this does


01:10:11.769 --> 01:10:16.012
is it gives a visual
representation of the usage.


01:10:16.019 --> 01:10:18.509
And they put it into the schools


01:10:18.518 --> 01:10:21.385
and so it's really gotten
a lot more into the community.


01:10:21.393 --> 01:10:25.386
And the teachers are
utilising it in their lessons.


01:10:25.393 --> 01:10:27.099
I want you to pay
close attention


01:10:27.101 --> 01:10:29.512
to the squirrel on
the right side.


01:10:29.517 --> 01:10:31.258
And watch what happens.


01:10:31.268 --> 01:10:36.843
As it goes down, he gets sadder
and sadder and starts to cry.


01:10:36.851 --> 01:10:39.262
Why do you think the
squirrel got happier or sadder?


01:10:39.267 --> 01:10:40.757
I noticed that too.


01:10:40.767 --> 01:10:42.257
Well, probably
because we're using


01:10:42.267 --> 01:10:44.132
a lot of electricity
at that point.


01:10:44.142 --> 01:10:46.097
More than we regularly used.


01:10:46.099 --> 01:10:47.590
We did a controlled study


01:10:47.600 --> 01:10:49.591
where we have
another school system


01:10:49.600 --> 01:10:51.715
very similar to oberlin's
school systems


01:10:51.724 --> 01:10:53.261
in a different area of Ohio.


01:10:53.267 --> 01:10:55.382
They've got
the same curriculum as we do,


01:10:55.391 --> 01:10:56.881
but they don't have dashboard.


01:10:56.891 --> 01:10:59.758
We found as a result of
exposure,


01:10:59.766 --> 01:11:02.632
if you ask them about
electricity use,


01:11:02.640 --> 01:11:05.383
they start talking about
emotions.


01:11:05.391 --> 01:11:08.006
And when you compare
students in our school


01:11:08.015 --> 01:11:09.597
with this other school,


01:11:09.599 --> 01:11:11.839
they also have more of
a community response


01:11:11.848 --> 01:11:15.136
when they talk about what needs
to be done about energy use.


01:11:15.140 --> 01:11:17.005
They use the word 'we'.


01:11:17.015 --> 01:11:20.257
And in the other school system,
they use the word 't'.


01:11:20.265 --> 01:11:22.381
So we've seen real change


01:11:22.390 --> 01:11:26.338
as a result of this technology
in the school.


01:11:29.764 --> 01:11:32.836
What is most exciting
about this idea


01:11:32.847 --> 01:11:34.883
is that the dashboards
could reconnect us


01:11:34.889 --> 01:11:36.470
to a range of resources...


01:11:38.264 --> 01:11:40.128
And influence our behaviour.


01:11:43.639 --> 01:11:45.594
It's a way to really
engage the community


01:11:45.596 --> 01:11:47.133
in this discussion,


01:11:47.139 --> 01:11:50.881
regardless of what race,
what education, economic level,


01:11:50.888 --> 01:11:52.753
in this discussion
on sustainability


01:11:52.763 --> 01:11:54.253
and the environment.


01:11:59.971 --> 01:12:02.382
But awareness of
our resource use


01:12:02.387 --> 01:12:04.753
is just one piece of
a complicated puzzle.


01:12:06.345 --> 01:12:07.835
Another solution


01:12:07.845 --> 01:12:10.382
that could have a huge impact
on our dilemma


01:12:10.387 --> 01:12:13.003
caught me completely off-guard.


01:12:16.345 --> 01:12:20.634
Probably our biggest
surprise was educating girls


01:12:20.637 --> 01:12:23.252
and family planning.


01:12:23.261 --> 01:12:24.876
You combine these two together,


01:12:24.886 --> 01:12:27.377
the number one solution
to reversing global warming


01:12:27.386 --> 01:12:29.217
is the empowerment
of girls and women.


01:12:29.219 --> 01:12:30.709
Wow.


01:12:38.260 --> 01:12:41.958
So, our daughter
starts school next year


01:12:41.969 --> 01:12:44.380
and as an Australian,


01:12:44.385 --> 01:12:47.127
I completely take for granted


01:12:47.135 --> 01:12:50.581
that she will get to complete
her education.


01:12:50.592 --> 01:12:52.584
And what I'm just learning here


01:12:52.593 --> 01:12:56.084
is that there are 65 million
girls around the world


01:12:56.092 --> 01:12:57.628
that don't get that opportunity.


01:12:58.842 --> 01:13:00.332
And there's a host of reasons
for that.


01:13:00.342 --> 01:13:02.879
Some are put to work to
support families


01:13:02.884 --> 01:13:04.374
or they're married off.


01:13:04.384 --> 01:13:05.874
But on average,


01:13:05.884 --> 01:13:08.842
those girls will have
five or more children.


01:13:11.591 --> 01:13:13.252
So we found
that across the board,


01:13:13.258 --> 01:13:14.749
and this isn't just countries


01:13:14.759 --> 01:13:16.248
where there might be
high rates of poverty.


01:13:16.258 --> 01:13:17.623
Even wealthier countries,


01:13:17.633 --> 01:13:20.625
we know that when girls
stay in school for longer


01:13:20.633 --> 01:13:23.749
and they have access to good
reproductive health services,


01:13:23.757 --> 01:13:26.340
as well as decent
work opportunities,


01:13:26.341 --> 01:13:29.002
that women will tend to delay
having children


01:13:29.007 --> 01:13:30.498
until they're ready,


01:13:30.508 --> 01:13:32.964
and then they choose to have
fewer children from then on.


01:13:32.965 --> 01:13:34.626
So, what this does
as a collective,


01:13:34.632 --> 01:13:37.339
is it slows down
the population growth rate,


01:13:37.340 --> 01:13:38.876
which means that
there's less pressure


01:13:38.882 --> 01:13:42.079
and competition for access to
a whole range of resources,


01:13:42.090 --> 01:13:44.251
whether it's food or water
or land.


01:13:45.881 --> 01:13:47.964
Their children tend to
stay in school longer as well


01:13:47.965 --> 01:13:51.752
and, over time, they break
that cycle of poverty.


01:13:55.881 --> 01:13:57.963
So it's a form of
family planning,


01:13:57.964 --> 01:13:59.500
but it's not coercive.


01:13:59.506 --> 01:14:00.962
It's not control.


01:14:00.964 --> 01:14:04.877
It's just empowering girls
to be who they want to be


01:14:04.881 --> 01:14:07.952
and you have
these incredible benefits.


01:14:14.255 --> 01:14:18.452
One child, one teacher,


01:14:18.462 --> 01:14:23.869
one book and one pen
can change the world.


01:14:46.336 --> 01:14:47.997
So this could be
a pleasant afternoon


01:14:48.003 --> 01:14:50.619
in a standard city of 2040.


01:14:57.586 --> 01:14:59.450
Freed-up space has encouraged


01:14:59.460 --> 01:15:02.247
a range of new
outdoor activities.


01:15:06.377 --> 01:15:10.325
Imagine, velvet, you and
your cool group of friends


01:15:10.335 --> 01:15:11.949
have met for a picnic.


01:15:13.626 --> 01:15:15.117
Although it is unlikely


01:15:15.127 --> 01:15:17.367
that Princess Elsa will still
be part of your crew.


01:15:21.751 --> 01:15:26.324
My hope is that we are all
using resources more wisely


01:15:26.334 --> 01:15:29.120
and some of the clever
innovations that exist today...


01:15:29.125 --> 01:15:30.616
Selfiel


01:15:30.626 --> 01:15:32.491
except for this drone phone
that I did make up


01:15:32.501 --> 01:15:35.615
<i>- to obliterate selfie sticks...
- Home!</i>


01:15:35.625 --> 01:15:37.616
Have been widely adopted.


01:15:40.250 --> 01:15:45.323
Some materials are grown
or made from organic matter.


01:15:50.874 --> 01:15:55.243
While other materials have been
recycled or break down rapidly.


01:15:58.332 --> 01:15:59.868
And wouldn't it be terrific


01:15:59.874 --> 01:16:01.829
if we have not only implemented
the smart ways


01:16:01.831 --> 01:16:04.369
to deal with the billions of
coffee cups


01:16:04.374 --> 01:16:06.079
we send to landfill each year...


01:16:08.123 --> 01:16:09.739
But each city of the world


01:16:09.749 --> 01:16:11.989
has followed the lead of
Stockholm,


01:16:11.998 --> 01:16:14.364
who collect their residents'
food waste...


01:16:16.623 --> 01:16:19.615
Extract the methane to
power their local vehicles...


01:16:21.248 --> 01:16:23.579
And give the leftovers
to farmers


01:16:23.580 --> 01:16:26.368
to use as a natural
fertiliser for their soils.


01:16:28.497 --> 01:16:31.489
<i>Well played, Sweden.
Well played.</i>


01:16:35.121 --> 01:16:37.738
These behaviour changes
in our resource use


01:16:37.747 --> 01:16:39.702
could be reflected on dashboards


01:16:39.704 --> 01:16:41.866
throughout our cities,


01:16:41.872 --> 01:16:43.611
where there used to be ads.


01:16:45.829 --> 01:16:48.366
Even encouraging some
healthy competition.


01:16:55.371 --> 01:16:59.990
But in this 2040,
my greatest hope


01:16:59.995 --> 01:17:02.361
is that girls across the globe


01:17:02.370 --> 01:17:05.737
now have access to the same
educational opportunities


01:17:05.745 --> 01:17:07.861
as you and your friends.


01:17:22.994 --> 01:17:25.360
The cascading benefits of this


01:17:25.369 --> 01:17:29.611
could be greater than anything
else I have discovered.


01:18:27.948 --> 01:18:31.987
So, velvet, this was my story


01:18:31.990 --> 01:18:35.062
of a future I hope
you get to experience.


01:18:36.323 --> 01:18:40.235
Your story and
the one that transpires


01:18:40.239 --> 01:18:41.855
may be completely different.


01:18:43.115 --> 01:18:45.070
But what I now understand


01:18:45.072 --> 01:18:47.485
is that not only are there
so many people


01:18:47.490 --> 01:18:50.356
who want to take part
in telling a new story...


01:18:52.072 --> 01:18:55.815
But we have everything
we need right now


01:18:55.822 --> 01:18:57.608
to make it happen.


01:19:00.863 --> 01:19:04.731
When you go beyond
the dominant media discourse


01:19:04.738 --> 01:19:06.979
and get closer to the ground,


01:19:06.988 --> 01:19:10.731
you will see,
everywhere you look,


01:19:10.738 --> 01:19:13.103
incredible reasons for hope.


01:19:23.237 --> 01:19:25.103
Over the next few years,


01:19:25.113 --> 01:19:27.603
you may notice less
red meat for dinner...


01:19:29.819 --> 01:19:31.606
More trips to the compost bin...


01:19:34.444 --> 01:19:36.106
Better search engines...


01:19:39.319 --> 01:19:42.482
And potentially a new
dashboard in your classroom.


01:19:45.361 --> 01:19:50.309
If enough of us make
these sorts of small changes,


01:19:50.318 --> 01:19:53.106
when combined,
they will make a difference.


01:19:54.860 --> 01:19:56.976
Big banks continue to take a hit


01:19:56.985 --> 01:20:00.182
as the public shifts its money
away from organisations


01:20:00.193 --> 01:20:02.104
that support fossil fuels.


01:20:04.068 --> 01:20:07.104
But of equal importance
is strong leadership.


01:20:09.735 --> 01:20:12.851
Wouldn't it be terrific
if new leaders emerge


01:20:12.859 --> 01:20:15.067
who can navigate us
to a better 20407?


01:20:18.859 --> 01:20:21.851
And just imagine if they used
the doughnut framework


01:20:21.859 --> 01:20:23.564
to create the new story...


01:20:24.859 --> 01:20:26.975
Coupled with more
and more people


01:20:26.984 --> 01:20:29.224
finding a defining role
within it.


01:20:31.484 --> 01:20:34.725
What we can do
is see where you get lit up


01:20:34.733 --> 01:20:36.439
and maybe it's
girls' education 


01:20:36.441 --> 01:20:37.977
"wow. I had no idea!"


01:20:38.983 --> 01:20:41.315
Or it can be food or it can be
farming or it could be energy,


01:20:41.316 --> 01:20:43.432
it could be housing, transport.


01:20:43.441 --> 01:20:45.351
I mean, there's so many areas


01:20:45.357 --> 01:20:48.975
where you actually probably
do have influence


01:20:48.982 --> 01:20:50.973
where you are working,


01:20:50.982 --> 01:20:53.223
where you can affect change


01:20:53.232 --> 01:20:55.723
in a way that actually
is demonstrable


01:20:55.732 --> 01:20:57.973
and meaningful
to you and others.


01:21:02.982 --> 01:21:06.303
And it just unleashes
the power of innovation


01:21:06.314 --> 01:21:10.353
and imagination and creativity
that's within all people.


01:21:17.980 --> 01:21:20.097
My hope for you, darling,


01:21:20.106 --> 01:21:24.220
is that sometime in
2040 there's a day


01:21:24.230 --> 01:21:26.972
when the greenhouse gases
in our atmosphere


01:21:26.980 --> 01:21:30.723
stop increasing and
begin to come down.


01:21:32.438 --> 01:21:34.928
And the solutions
that achieve this


01:21:34.937 --> 01:21:37.350
benefit a wide range of people.


01:21:42.604 --> 01:21:46.302
To celebrate, there are parties
across the globe


01:21:46.313 --> 01:21:48.473
for you and your generation.


01:21:49.854 --> 01:21:51.434
The regeneration.


01:22:33.059 --> 01:22:36.972
When I grow up, I want homeless
people to have their own homes


01:22:36.976 --> 01:22:39.342
with their own money
to buy their own food


01:22:39.351 --> 01:22:40.841
and have their own jobs.


01:22:41.934 --> 01:22:45.425
Well, I'd like it
to be human instinct


01:22:45.433 --> 01:22:48.971
to just look after the world
and to care for the world.


01:22:48.975 --> 01:22:51.307
I would like to see everybody
have equal rights


01:22:51.308 --> 01:22:53.219
and the same living
opportunities.


01:22:54.225 --> 01:22:56.716
I just want the future
to be good.


01:23:06.224 --> 01:23:07.931
Dadda!


01:23:18.598 --> 01:23:20.089
So...


01:23:21.849 --> 01:23:23.588
What's your 2040?


01:25:34.799 --> 01:25:38.792
I want to see clean
air for more people to breathe


01:25:38.799 --> 01:25:40.585
and clean air, so that the trees


01:25:40.591 --> 01:25:44.788
don't have to take the smoke
into their leaves and die.


01:25:47.341 --> 01:25:51.208
I also hope that we'll
be able to solve world hunger,


01:25:51.215 --> 01:25:53.331
'cause a lot of people
are going hungry.


01:25:54.340 --> 01:25:56.330
At the moment,
the ice caps are melting


01:25:56.339 --> 01:25:58.456
because of all
the greenhouse gases.


01:26:00.089 --> 01:26:02.832
# the earth speaks
loud and clear #


01:26:02.840 --> 01:26:05.796
# the time to act is here #


01:26:05.797 --> 01:26:07.207
I have want to make sure


01:26:07.214 --> 01:26:08.954
there's no pollution
in the ocean.


01:26:12.089 --> 01:26:14.580
# tomorrow's become today #


01:26:14.589 --> 01:26:18.035
# it's too late to turn away #


01:26:19.089 --> 01:26:23.286
I want to see fresh air,
fresh water, no pollution.


01:26:23.296 --> 01:26:26.413
Because that will kill animals
and I want no animals to die.


01:26:28.088 --> 01:26:30.920
I'd like it
to be human instinct


01:26:30.921 --> 01:26:35.085
to just look after the world
and to care for the world.


01:26:35.088 --> 01:26:36.828
I would like
to see a world


01:26:36.838 --> 01:26:40.079
where people live in peace,
Harmony and happiness


01:26:40.087 --> 01:26:43.329
and not a world
where people live in fear.


01:26:43.337 --> 01:26:44.952
I don't think
we should use guns


01:26:44.962 --> 01:26:46.577
and force each other around.


01:26:50.836 --> 01:26:53.419
# the creatures we have lost #


01:27:02.586 --> 01:27:06.204
# so please don't turn away #


01:27:06.211 --> 01:27:09.829
In the future, I'd like
to see more beautiful things


01:27:09.836 --> 01:27:12.417
like flowers, more trees.


01:27:12.418 --> 01:27:13.909
Cleaner water.


01:27:13.919 --> 01:27:15.328
Be respectful to earth.


01:27:15.335 --> 01:27:16.950
Less global warming.


01:27:16.960 --> 01:27:20.908
We could stop littering
and make sure coral looks ok


01:27:20.918 --> 01:27:23.910
'cause it normally looks
like a rainbow in the sea.


01:27:26.710 --> 01:27:29.417
# so please don't turn away #


01:27:29.418 --> 01:27:31.204
And also,
I'd like my kids


01:27:31.210 --> 01:27:35.032
to be able to swim
in fresh water.


01:27:38.792 --> 01:27:42.330
# so please don't turn away #


01:27:42.334 --> 01:27:44.073
A lot more inventions.


01:27:44.083 --> 01:27:45.699
Electric cars.


01:27:45.709 --> 01:27:47.791
Take transportation.


01:27:50.666 --> 01:27:54.534
# so please don't turn away #


01:28:15.582 --> 01:28:18.414
If people keep doing
what they're doing now,


01:28:18.415 --> 01:28:21.202
the world won't be
a very good place.


01:28:23.915 --> 01:28:29.822
# so please don't
turn away the future #


01:28:29.831 --> 01:28:31.287
I would also like


01:28:31.289 --> 01:28:33.825
if money was
more evenly distributed.


01:28:33.830 --> 01:28:36.197
I would like to see
everybody have equal rights


01:28:36.206 --> 01:28:38.287
and the same living
opportunities.


01:28:38.288 --> 01:28:41.031
For the government
to have done something


01:28:41.039 --> 01:28:43.700
on global warming and pollution.


01:28:48.330 --> 01:28:51.527
I want people to treat
each other better.

